WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.448 --> 00:00:02.865
(soft music)

00:00:06.430 --> 00:00:08.540
- My name is Dr. Sabrina Smiley.

00:00:08.540 --> 00:00:12.500
I'm one of the 2022 program co-chairs,

00:00:12.500 --> 00:00:16.123
and we are so excited to
host this evening's event.

00:00:18.450 --> 00:00:21.500
The topic tonight is
tobacco regulatory science,

00:00:21.500 --> 00:00:25.300
health equity, mental health,
and public engagement.

00:00:25.300 --> 00:00:30.300
And it's a live podcast
created by Dr. Nii Addy.

00:00:30.520 --> 00:00:32.740
And we have an expert panel,

00:00:32.740 --> 00:00:37.053
Dr. Pebbles Fagan, Mitch
Zeller, and Kathleen Crosby.

00:00:38.170 --> 00:00:41.653
I'm gonna introduce Dr.
Addy and let him have it.

00:00:43.450 --> 00:00:46.140
Dr. Nii Addy is the
Albert E. Kent Professor

00:00:46.140 --> 00:00:48.490
of Psychiatry and Associate Professor

00:00:48.490 --> 00:00:51.300
of Cellular and Molecular Physiology.

00:00:51.300 --> 00:00:54.950
He received his BS in
Biology from Duke University

00:00:54.950 --> 00:00:59.270
and his PhD in Neuroscience
from Yale University.

00:00:59.270 --> 00:01:02.710
Dr. Addy directs a
federally-funded research program

00:01:02.710 --> 00:01:05.360
investigating cholinergic, dopaminergic

00:01:05.360 --> 00:01:08.410
and L-type calcium channel mechanisms

00:01:08.410 --> 00:01:11.940
mediating substance
use and mood disorders.

00:01:11.940 --> 00:01:14.580
Dr. Addy's team also studies the ability

00:01:14.580 --> 00:01:16.690
of tobacco product flavor additives

00:01:16.690 --> 00:01:20.420
to alter nicotine use
behavior and addiction.

00:01:20.420 --> 00:01:22.700
He serves on the journal editorial board

00:01:22.700 --> 00:01:26.897
of "Neuropsychopharmacology,
Biological Psychiatry,

00:01:26.897 --> 00:01:31.370
"Nicotine and Tobacco Research,
and Neuropharmacology,"

00:01:31.370 --> 00:01:33.960
and as a grant reviewer
for the Neurobiology

00:01:33.960 --> 00:01:36.490
of Motivated Behavior Study Section

00:01:36.490 --> 00:01:38.790
of the National Institutes
of Health's Center

00:01:38.790 --> 00:01:40.163
for Scientific Review.

00:01:41.220 --> 00:01:44.550
Dr. Addy currently serves
as the inaugural director

00:01:44.550 --> 00:01:46.810
of Scientist Diversity and Inclusion

00:01:46.810 --> 00:01:48.990
at the Yale School of Medicine,

00:01:48.990 --> 00:01:51.620
as the director of the
Faculty Mentoring Program

00:01:51.620 --> 00:01:53.510
for the Yale Minority Organization

00:01:53.510 --> 00:01:57.460
for Recruitment and
Expansion, also known as MORE,

00:01:57.460 --> 00:02:00.460
and as co-chair of the Career
Development Subcommittee

00:02:00.460 --> 00:02:02.730
of the Anti-Racism Task Force

00:02:02.730 --> 00:02:05.420
in the Yale Department of Psychiatry.

00:02:05.420 --> 00:02:07.830
He also contributes to graduate student

00:02:07.830 --> 00:02:09.460
and postdoctoral training

00:02:09.460 --> 00:02:13.160
and to diversity, equity
and inclusion initiatives

00:02:13.160 --> 00:02:17.460
through his efforts on campus
and in scientific societies.

00:02:17.460 --> 00:02:21.650
So without further ado,
please welcome Dr. Addy.

00:02:21.650 --> 00:02:24.650
(audience applauds)

00:02:26.290 --> 00:02:27.970
- Thank you very much
for that introduction.

00:02:27.970 --> 00:02:29.270
It's always humbling to hear.

00:02:29.270 --> 00:02:30.330
And every time I hear it,

00:02:30.330 --> 00:02:32.290
I always have to emphasize all the teams

00:02:32.290 --> 00:02:34.930
that are actually in place
to help us do this work.

00:02:34.930 --> 00:02:37.490
So I know that's been a
theme of the conference

00:02:37.490 --> 00:02:39.890
for all those of you who
are here in the room.

00:02:39.890 --> 00:02:41.890
But I'm really grateful to be
able to have this opportunity

00:02:41.890 --> 00:02:43.870
to have our first in-person recording

00:02:43.870 --> 00:02:45.960
of the Addy Hour podcast.

00:02:45.960 --> 00:02:47.580
There's definitely a
different level of energy

00:02:47.580 --> 00:02:49.110
that just comes from being in the room.

00:02:49.110 --> 00:02:51.673
I'm sure our listeners who'll
be listening across platforms

00:02:51.673 --> 00:02:54.570
will be able to sense that as well.

00:02:54.570 --> 00:02:57.020
So in the spirit of where we are in time,

00:02:57.020 --> 00:02:59.410
I just want to open up and
kind of set the tone to say,

00:02:59.410 --> 00:03:01.040
as others have said,
you know, this is a time

00:03:01.040 --> 00:03:03.530
where we're definitely
celebrating a lot of things,

00:03:03.530 --> 00:03:05.390
being able to move and engage in ways

00:03:05.390 --> 00:03:07.877
that we haven't done for
quite a while as a society,

00:03:07.877 --> 00:03:10.840
being able to celebrate a lot
of the work that's being done

00:03:10.840 --> 00:03:14.330
around tobacco regulatory science
as we'll talk about today.

00:03:14.330 --> 00:03:15.163
But at the same time,

00:03:15.163 --> 00:03:17.650
we know there's still a
lot that has to be done.

00:03:17.650 --> 00:03:19.660
And that's why I think
it's entirely appropriate

00:03:19.660 --> 00:03:22.520
and possible for us to hold
both of those things together

00:03:22.520 --> 00:03:25.880
as we continue to move
forward as a society.

00:03:25.880 --> 00:03:28.360
And I think what we'll be
talking about today also reflects

00:03:28.360 --> 00:03:30.680
what's going on in society at large.

00:03:30.680 --> 00:03:32.590
Obviously there's a lot
of challenges going on

00:03:32.590 --> 00:03:34.350
in the world today, even as we think

00:03:34.350 --> 00:03:37.400
about everything that's happening
in Ukraine and elsewhere.

00:03:37.400 --> 00:03:41.500
I also did want to acknowledge
that this is March 16th, 2022

00:03:41.500 --> 00:03:42.360
when we're recording.

00:03:42.360 --> 00:03:45.960
And so this is the one-year
anniversary of the tragic events

00:03:45.960 --> 00:03:48.350
that happened in Atlanta a year ago

00:03:48.350 --> 00:03:51.650
when several lives were
tragically lost from our Asian,

00:03:51.650 --> 00:03:53.690
Asian-American, Pacific
Islander community.

00:03:53.690 --> 00:03:56.397
So I want to make sure that
we least acknowledge that

00:03:56.397 --> 00:03:58.580
and continue to give space for that

00:03:58.580 --> 00:04:00.050
and not forget about the those things.

00:04:00.050 --> 00:04:03.080
Because even though that
was one isolated incident,

00:04:03.080 --> 00:04:05.260
there are other things
that continue to happen

00:04:05.260 --> 00:04:06.597
to members of that community.

00:04:06.597 --> 00:04:08.080
And we wanna make sure that we continue

00:04:08.080 --> 00:04:09.570
to elevate those conversations

00:04:09.570 --> 00:04:12.200
and to move things forward to action.

00:04:12.200 --> 00:04:14.100
And of course, that action
is something I think

00:04:14.100 --> 00:04:16.060
that can give us hope
as we continue to try

00:04:16.060 --> 00:04:17.370
and move forward as a society,

00:04:17.370 --> 00:04:18.590
even with all the other things

00:04:18.590 --> 00:04:20.120
that we've been moving forward,

00:04:20.120 --> 00:04:23.360
some of those things which
you'll hear about today as well.

00:04:23.360 --> 00:04:26.530
So with that framing and
that approach, in a sense,

00:04:26.530 --> 00:04:28.837
I'm really grateful and
honored to be able to be here

00:04:28.837 --> 00:04:30.360
and to host this conversation

00:04:30.360 --> 00:04:31.980
with all of our esteemed guests.

00:04:31.980 --> 00:04:35.590
I want to go ahead and
introduce them to you now.

00:04:35.590 --> 00:04:37.100
So as was mentioned already,

00:04:37.100 --> 00:04:38.960
the topic for today's discussion

00:04:38.960 --> 00:04:41.480
will be tobacco regulatory science,

00:04:41.480 --> 00:04:44.740
health equity, mental health,
and public engagement.

00:04:44.740 --> 00:04:47.100
And so I'm honored to be
able to host individuals

00:04:47.100 --> 00:04:49.230
who have been in the forefront
of those types of efforts

00:04:49.230 --> 00:04:52.250
over the years, and excited
to also have them interact

00:04:52.250 --> 00:04:53.280
with each other

00:04:53.280 --> 00:04:57.020
and to see how we can even
elevate that conversation

00:04:57.020 --> 00:04:59.010
and continue to move things forward.

00:04:59.010 --> 00:05:01.910
So I think what I will do
is actually just introduce

00:05:01.910 --> 00:05:04.390
in the order that we have
of everyone sitting here

00:05:04.390 --> 00:05:05.700
on the stage.

00:05:05.700 --> 00:05:08.640
So the first guest with which
I would like to introduce,

00:05:08.640 --> 00:05:10.820
which is someone who's
familiar to those of you

00:05:10.820 --> 00:05:13.380
who are here in person is Mitch Zeller,

00:05:13.380 --> 00:05:16.680
who is the director of the
Center for Tobacco Products.

00:05:16.680 --> 00:05:19.140
And in that role, he leads FDA's efforts

00:05:19.140 --> 00:05:22.080
to reduce disease and
death from tobacco use

00:05:22.080 --> 00:05:24.290
and bring previously
unavailable information

00:05:24.290 --> 00:05:26.560
about its dangers to light.

00:05:26.560 --> 00:05:30.400
Mitch has been in this role at
the FDA since March of 2013.

00:05:30.400 --> 00:05:31.233
And in that role,

00:05:31.233 --> 00:05:33.980
he has been implementing the mission

00:05:33.980 --> 00:05:35.453
of the Center for Tobacco Products

00:05:35.453 --> 00:05:38.150
to make tobacco-related death and disease

00:05:38.150 --> 00:05:41.200
part of America's past
and not America's future,

00:05:41.200 --> 00:05:43.860
and by doing so, to
ensure a healthier life

00:05:43.860 --> 00:05:46.106
for every American family.

00:05:46.106 --> 00:05:48.520
Mitch is someone who has
been at the forefront

00:05:48.520 --> 00:05:52.160
and working at FDA-related
issues for more than 30 years.

00:05:52.160 --> 00:05:54.660
He actually began his career
as a public interest attorney

00:05:54.660 --> 00:05:58.502
in 1982 at the Center for
Science in the Public Interest.

00:05:58.502 --> 00:06:01.230
In 1993, he actually joined the FDA

00:06:01.230 --> 00:06:03.900
under then-commissioner Dr. David Kessler,

00:06:03.900 --> 00:06:06.270
and what was supposed to
be a two-week assignment

00:06:06.270 --> 00:06:09.866
actually turned into a much
longer foray with the country,

00:06:09.866 --> 00:06:12.613
with the FDA, so to speak,

00:06:12.613 --> 00:06:15.320
where he was serving as
an associate commissioner

00:06:15.320 --> 00:06:20.220
and director of the FDA's first
Office of Tobacco Programs.

00:06:20.220 --> 00:06:22.050
He actually left the FDA in 2000

00:06:22.050 --> 00:06:23.910
to continue to work in this space

00:06:23.910 --> 00:06:26.310
with the American Legacy Foundation,

00:06:26.310 --> 00:06:28.470
also had a stint as senior vice president

00:06:28.470 --> 00:06:29.460
of Pinney Associates,

00:06:29.460 --> 00:06:32.457
and then again returned to FDA in 2013,

00:06:32.457 --> 00:06:35.060
and as many of you know
will be retiring soon.

00:06:35.060 --> 00:06:38.050
So this has been in some ways
a celebration of his efforts,

00:06:38.050 --> 00:06:38.940
and I'm honored and grateful

00:06:38.940 --> 00:06:40.690
to be able to welcome Mitch Zeller.

00:06:41.943 --> 00:06:42.880
- Thank you.

00:06:42.880 --> 00:06:46.270
- And seated next to Mitch
is Dr. Pebbles Fagan,

00:06:46.270 --> 00:06:47.830
who has lots of different titles.

00:06:47.830 --> 00:06:50.070
I'm only gonna highlight
a few of them here,

00:06:50.070 --> 00:06:51.760
just for the sake of time.

00:06:51.760 --> 00:06:53.980
Dr. Fagan is a professor and the director

00:06:53.980 --> 00:06:55.870
of the Center for the Study of Tobacco

00:06:55.870 --> 00:06:59.200
in the Fay W. Boozman
College of Public Health.

00:06:59.200 --> 00:07:01.080
She's also the director of research

00:07:01.080 --> 00:07:03.520
in the Office of Health Initiatives
and Disparities Research

00:07:03.520 --> 00:07:06.550
in the College of Medicine

00:07:06.550 --> 00:07:08.470
at the University of Arkansas.

00:07:08.470 --> 00:07:10.610
And she's someone who has
been engaged in this work

00:07:10.610 --> 00:07:13.260
for over 25 years conducting research

00:07:13.260 --> 00:07:15.360
that seeks to understand
factors associated

00:07:15.360 --> 00:07:19.550
with tobacco use and exposure
in racial and ethnic women,

00:07:19.550 --> 00:07:23.580
low-socioeconomic status and
youth, young adult populations.

00:07:23.580 --> 00:07:26.170
And she also seeks to develop
community interventions

00:07:26.170 --> 00:07:30.120
that aim to reduce tobacco and
cancer-related disparities.

00:07:30.120 --> 00:07:33.600
Dr. Fagan has a number of
leadership roles and initiatives.

00:07:33.600 --> 00:07:34.490
We'll now mention this,

00:07:34.490 --> 00:07:36.190
the newly funded Center for Research,

00:07:36.190 --> 00:07:38.083
Health and Social Justice

00:07:38.083 --> 00:07:41.314
She also is a senior advisor to the CDC

00:07:41.314 --> 00:07:43.780
and also has put a lot
of time and investment

00:07:43.780 --> 00:07:45.710
to training students and faculty members

00:07:45.710 --> 00:07:48.350
to advance their research careers as well.

00:07:48.350 --> 00:07:49.850
Again, several different accolades,

00:07:49.850 --> 00:07:50.930
one I will mention.

00:07:50.930 --> 00:07:53.110
So those of you in the
room have heard this,

00:07:53.110 --> 00:07:55.060
those listening, I also want
to make sure that you know

00:07:55.060 --> 00:07:57.650
that she just received
the presidential award

00:07:57.650 --> 00:08:00.050
from the Society for Research
on Nicotine and Tobacco

00:08:00.050 --> 00:08:01.970
for her career contributions

00:08:01.970 --> 00:08:04.450
to tobacco and disparities research.

00:08:04.450 --> 00:08:07.563
So honored to welcome Dr.
Pebbles Fagan to the Addy Hour.

00:08:09.540 --> 00:08:11.420
And last, but definitely not least,

00:08:11.420 --> 00:08:13.830
also would like to
introduce Kathleen Crosby,

00:08:13.830 --> 00:08:15.630
who is currently the
director of the Office

00:08:15.630 --> 00:08:18.040
of Health Communication and Education

00:08:18.040 --> 00:08:20.810
at the FDA Center for Tobacco Products.

00:08:20.810 --> 00:08:21.643
So in that role,

00:08:21.643 --> 00:08:23.400
she leads an office that is responsible

00:08:23.400 --> 00:08:25.020
for conducting public education,

00:08:25.020 --> 00:08:28.100
stakeholder outreach and
regulatory communication programs

00:08:28.100 --> 00:08:30.170
designed to ensure FDA success

00:08:30.170 --> 00:08:32.870
in implementing the Tobacco Control Act.

00:08:32.870 --> 00:08:34.160
These efforts include developing

00:08:34.160 --> 00:08:35.820
breakthrough communication strategies

00:08:35.820 --> 00:08:37.670
to reduce youth tobacco use,

00:08:37.670 --> 00:08:39.409
helping current tobacco users quit

00:08:39.409 --> 00:08:42.650
and building stakeholder
understanding of compliance

00:08:42.650 --> 00:08:45.430
and FDA product regulations.

00:08:45.430 --> 00:08:48.230
She's been involved in multiple
public education campaigns,

00:08:48.230 --> 00:08:50.160
including The Real Cost.

00:08:50.160 --> 00:08:53.560
And she also has a longstanding
career with over 20 years

00:08:53.560 --> 00:08:57.430
of experience in senior level
marketing and advertising

00:08:57.430 --> 00:09:01.090
and working on several large
scale multimedia campaigns,

00:09:01.090 --> 00:09:03.930
also served at the Ad
Council for quite a while,

00:09:03.930 --> 00:09:06.110
and was a campaign director

00:09:06.110 --> 00:09:08.820
for 17 United States government agencies,

00:09:08.820 --> 00:09:11.280
someone who's been very
invested in this realm

00:09:11.280 --> 00:09:12.113
for quite some time,

00:09:12.113 --> 00:09:14.070
and I'm glad to be able
to welcome her here

00:09:14.070 --> 00:09:15.223
to the podcast as well.

00:09:16.640 --> 00:09:18.020
Well, enough of me speaking.

00:09:18.020 --> 00:09:20.680
We obviously have folks
who have lots of accolades

00:09:20.680 --> 00:09:22.919
