over the last couple of
years and moving forward,

00:11:50.550 --> 00:11:52.360
just using those opportunities

00:11:52.360 --> 00:11:54.510
to really move things
forward as is needed.

00:11:55.370 --> 00:11:56.470
Mitch, what about you?

00:11:57.639 --> 00:12:01.580
- It has been great to be
with a bunch of human beings

00:12:01.580 --> 00:12:02.413
in person.

00:12:03.600 --> 00:12:06.743
I said it when I took the
podium yesterday afternoon.

00:12:08.335 --> 00:12:13.335
There's gonna be some emerging definition

00:12:13.990 --> 00:12:15.840
of what the new normal is

00:12:15.840 --> 00:12:17.760
for all of us personally
and professionally.

00:12:17.760 --> 00:12:18.870
We don't know what that is yet.

00:12:18.870 --> 00:12:20.590
I think we all personally

00:12:20.590 --> 00:12:22.370
and professionally need
to expect the unexpected,

00:12:22.370 --> 00:12:25.610
whether it's the next surge
coming from the other side

00:12:25.610 --> 00:12:30.033
of the pond or other global issues.

00:12:31.180 --> 00:12:36.180
And for me, just on the professional side,

00:12:37.459 --> 00:12:41.420
it's been an extraordinary test

00:12:41.420 --> 00:12:46.160
of the people of the Center
for Tobacco Products at FDA.

00:12:46.160 --> 00:12:51.160
And I am grateful for the level of effort

00:12:52.220 --> 00:12:54.410
during unprecedented times.

00:12:54.410 --> 00:12:56.070
It has come at a price though,

00:12:56.070 --> 00:12:59.230
for all of us, not just those
of us in federal service.

00:12:59.230 --> 00:13:02.390
I mean, the challenge
of work-life balance,

00:13:02.390 --> 00:13:05.960
especially if your family
situation involves childcare

00:13:05.960 --> 00:13:07.250
or elder care

00:13:07.250 --> 00:13:10.563
or any other health-related issues.

00:13:12.403 --> 00:13:17.403
We've paid a price, but
it's been very gratifying

00:13:19.950 --> 00:13:23.640
to see the commitment and
the dedication of the people

00:13:23.640 --> 00:13:27.811
that I work with as we all kind
of have muddled through this

00:13:27.811 --> 00:13:31.580
from our home offices or
wherever we're working from,

00:13:31.580 --> 00:13:34.440
at home to get the job done.

00:13:34.440 --> 00:13:36.350
And I know that that goes
for everybody involved

00:13:36.350 --> 00:13:38.320
in tobacco control.

00:13:38.320 --> 00:13:41.113
And that to me is what's so,

00:13:42.260 --> 00:13:45.890
was the emotional reaction
I had to be here yesterday.

00:13:45.890 --> 00:13:49.800
Because we all have day
jobs in different realms,

00:13:49.800 --> 00:13:51.360
different sectors.

00:13:51.360 --> 00:13:53.560
We're all working towards the same

00:13:53.560 --> 00:13:54.870
overarching public health goal

00:13:54.870 --> 00:13:57.100
when it comes to tobacco use

00:13:57.100 --> 00:14:00.500
and to be back together
with the wonderful family

00:14:00.500 --> 00:14:02.930
of the Society for Research
on Nicotine and Tobacco

00:14:02.930 --> 00:14:06.150
meant a lot to me personally
and professionally.

00:14:06.150 --> 00:14:06.983
- That's great.

00:14:06.983 --> 00:14:09.080
Thanks so much for sharing
that, really well said.

00:14:09.080 --> 00:14:10.940
And already, I know we're
only a few minutes in,

00:14:10.940 --> 00:14:15.020
but so many themes that are
cutting across all the comments

00:14:15.020 --> 00:14:16.310
that you all have made.

00:14:16.310 --> 00:14:18.600
One thing that really strikes
me about the work that you do

00:14:18.600 --> 00:14:22.500
is it's so focused on
people and on society,

00:14:22.500 --> 00:14:23.720
but then again, at some level,

00:14:23.720 --> 00:14:25.410
it seems that the level of humanity

00:14:25.410 --> 00:14:28.530
has actually been increased
over the last couple of years,

00:14:28.530 --> 00:14:31.010
as you're even talking
about being attentive

00:14:31.010 --> 00:14:32.100
to the different challenges

00:14:32.100 --> 00:14:34.510
that people have been walking through,

00:14:34.510 --> 00:14:35.810
Pebbles, as you're talking about looking

00:14:35.810 --> 00:14:37.780
for new opportunities,

00:14:37.780 --> 00:14:39.630
and Kathy, as you were
talking about, hope.

00:14:39.630 --> 00:14:42.250
So I wondered if you all
would just actually comment

00:14:42.250 --> 00:14:43.970
on that a little bit in the sense

00:14:43.970 --> 00:14:46.830
that how has that humanity been elevated

00:14:46.830 --> 00:14:49.360
in ways that it wasn't there before?

00:14:49.360 --> 00:14:51.580
'Cause again, it's something
you all were focused on before,

00:14:51.580 --> 00:14:53.340
but I get the sense that
there's a different level

00:14:53.340 --> 00:14:55.890
at which that has come across recently.

00:14:55.890 --> 00:14:57.430
So anyone who wants to jump-

00:14:57.430 --> 00:14:58.270
- I'll start.

00:14:58.270 --> 00:15:03.270
I think that with the systemic issues

00:15:03.880 --> 00:15:08.360
related to social justice and inequities

00:15:08.360 --> 00:15:10.100
that have always been there,

00:15:10.100 --> 00:15:14.250
but coincidentally emerged
during the last couple of years

00:15:14.250 --> 00:15:16.790
at a societal level, I sense all

00:15:16.790 --> 00:15:18.680
of these things coming together,

00:15:18.680 --> 00:15:22.470
because those kinds of systemic issues

00:15:22.470 --> 00:15:25.420
have a direct connection to
reducing the death and disease

00:15:25.420 --> 00:15:26.680
from tobacco use.

00:15:26.680 --> 00:15:27.640
Because when we look at,

00:15:27.640 --> 00:15:29.130
especially when we're talking

00:15:29.130 --> 00:15:30.840
about the most harmful products,

00:15:30.840 --> 00:15:33.440
name your combustible product,

00:15:33.440 --> 00:15:35.013
who is the remaining smoker?

00:15:35.890 --> 00:15:38.840
Who have we left behind
at a population level

00:15:38.840 --> 00:15:40.680
with all the progress that has been made

00:15:40.680 --> 00:15:41.720
in the last half century

00:15:41.720 --> 00:15:43.370
in reducing consumption and prevalence?

00:15:43.370 --> 00:15:45.980
And there's a direct
connection between all

00:15:45.980 --> 00:15:49.630
of the profound issues
relating to inequality

00:15:49.630 --> 00:15:53.000
in this country and the
health issue that we work on.

00:15:53.000 --> 00:15:56.020
So I've seen more of an intersection

00:15:56.020 --> 00:15:59.060
between all of that, unfortunately,

00:15:59.060 --> 00:16:02.100
because of tragedies that have taken place

00:16:02.100 --> 00:16:04.020
in some of our cities over
the last couple years.

00:16:04.020 --> 00:16:06.360
But from that,

00:16:06.360 --> 00:16:08.860
just to look at it through
the public health lens,

00:16:08.860 --> 00:16:09.950
is an opportunity,

00:16:09.950 --> 00:16:14.950
because the issue of health
equity and health disparity,

00:16:15.280 --> 00:16:19.380
in our field at least has
never been more top of mind

00:16:19.380 --> 00:16:23.210
and more prominent because of
what's going on in society,

00:16:23.210 --> 00:16:25.300
at least in my experience
in tobacco control,

00:16:25.300 --> 00:16:27.260
than I've seen in the last two years.

00:16:27.260 --> 00:16:30.920
And while there's huge,
underlying systemic problems

00:16:30.920 --> 00:16:33.650
that go beyond reducing
the death and disease

00:16:33.650 --> 00:16:36.810
from tobacco use, I can tell you,

00:16:36.810 --> 00:16:39.210
in this administration
in the federal government

00:16:40.160 --> 00:16:42.650
the issue of health equity
and health disparity

00:16:42.650 --> 00:16:44.500
is an enormous priority.

00:16:44.500 --> 00:16:48.330
And from that, comes this
huge public health opportunity

00:16:48.330 --> 00:16:49.690
for all of us

00:16:49.690 --> 00:16:52.090
when we think about who
the remaining smoker is.

00:16:53.960 --> 00:16:54.793
- Yeah.

00:16:54.793 --> 00:16:56.090
And when you think about humanity, I mean,

00:16:56.090 --> 00:16:59.360
of course COVID has forced
us, and not just COVID,

00:16:59.360 --> 00:17:02.010
but all of the social
injustices that have happened

00:17:02.010 --> 00:17:06.690
over the last several years
have forced us to think about,

00:17:06.690 --> 00:17:09.910
what does it mean to
have a humane society?

00:17:09.910 --> 00:17:14.910
And, you know, we have used
the social ecological model

00:17:16.110 --> 00:17:20.760
for many, many years to
understand the multiple influences

00:17:20.760 --> 00:17:24.011
on human behaviors and human development,

00:17:24.011 --> 00:17:27.960
but have done very little
on how we intervene

00:17:27.960 --> 00:17:31.980
on those factors beyond
the individual level.

00:17:31.980 --> 00:17:35.580
And so it has certainly forced me

00:17:35.580 --> 00:17:39.003
to think more about that, I've
always thought about that.

00:17:39.950 --> 00:17:41.540
And I can remember when I was

00:17:41.540 --> 00:17:45.160
at the National Cancer Institute
and I asked a question,

00:17:45.160 --> 00:17:47.560
why don't we do interventions

00:17:47.560 --> 00:17:49.380
that intervene on social factors?

00:17:49.380 --> 00:17:51.303
I was told NIH doesn't do that.

00:17:52.150 --> 00:17:56.683
And, but why don't we,
when the fact is that,

00:17:56.683 --> 00:17:59.600
the people who are left behind

00:17:59.600 --> 00:18:02.926
in terms of smoking are
people who are poor,

00:18:02.926 --> 00:18:05.980
people who may have low literacy,

00:18:05.980 --> 00:18:10.880
people who are geographically
isolated in rural communities,

00:18:10.880 --> 00:18:14.460
people who may live in
segregated neighborhoods,

00:18:14.460 --> 00:18:15.900
who may be racialized.

00:18:15.900 --> 00:18:19.220
And so when you think about those things,

00:18:19.220 --> 00:18:22.258
where tobacco use is still clustered

00:18:22.258 --> 00:18:24.840
and people are left behind,

00:18:24.840 --> 00:18:29.110
you have to begin to think
about the social circumstances

00:18:29.110 --> 00:18:30.880
and the structural factors

00:18:30.880 --> 00:18:33.540
and how do we shift and
change those things?

00:18:33.540 --> 00:18:36.580
And we started integrating those things

00:18:36.580 --> 00:18:39.250
into a randomized trial that I was doing,

00:18:39.250 --> 00:18:43.163
because our smokers in
our study were hungry.

00:18:44.063 --> 00:18:46.980
I mean, they were suffering
from food insecurity,

00:18:46.980 --> 00:18:50.770
and we had to address that issue.

00:18:50.770 --> 00:18:52.470
We could not just sit there

00:18:52.470 --> 00:18:56.410
and try to do motivational counseling

00:18:56.410 --> 00:19:00.660
and help people to keep their
homes smoke free and quit

00:19:00.660 --> 00:19:04.060
when they were suffering
from food insecurity issues.

00:19:04.060 --> 00:19:06.520
And so we had to begin to address that,

00:19:06.520 --> 00:19:08.360
partnering with the food bank

00:19:08.360 --> 00:19:10.150
and getting food out to the communities.

00:19:10.150 --> 00:19:11.400
That's what we had to do.

00:19:12.330 --> 00:19:15.060
- Yeah, really well said,
and really important.

00:19:15.060 --> 00:19:16.870
Before Kathy, before we jump to you,

00:19:16.870 --> 00:19:18.110
Dr. Fagan, I also just wanted to,

00:19:18.110 --> 00:19:19.410
and you started to elude to this already.

00:19:19.410 --> 00:19:22.420
As someone who's been doing
this work for so long,

00:19:22.420 --> 00:19:23.850
I mean, as much as
you're willing to share,

00:19:23.850 --> 00:19:26.250
how has this increased attention,

00:19:26.250 --> 00:19:29.319
how have you responded to that in a sense?

00:19:29.319 --> 00:19:31.447
I would imagine in some
ways that can be empowering,

00:19:31.447 --> 00:19:34.110
but I also imagine that
that's a mixed reaction,

00:19:34.110 --> 00:19:36.320
'cause I know I've had some
of those reactions myself.

00:19:36.320 --> 00:19:37.883
But how has that actually,

00:19:38.920 --> 00:19:39.753
one, how have you respond to that

00:19:39.753 --> 00:19:42.260
and how has that actually
impacted your work as well,

00:19:42.260 --> 00:19:44.504
as much as you wanna share?

00:19:44.504 --> 00:19:47.400
- Well, thanks for asking that question.

00:19:47.400 --> 00:19:51.520
When we had to start
doing food distributions

00:19:51.520 --> 00:19:52.730
in the community,

00:19:52.730 --> 00:19:55.160
we decided we would write another grant

00:19:55.160 --> 00:19:58.425
and test out whether or not
increasing food security

00:19:58.425 --> 00:20:00.600
has an impact on smoking cessation.

00:20:00.600 --> 00:20:01.950
That grant got funded.

00:20:01.950 --> 00:20:04.260
And that's part of our Center

00:20:04.260 --> 00:20:06.700
for Research Health and Social Justice.

00:20:06.700 --> 00:20:09.630
And so we are asking the question,

00:20:09.630 --> 00:20:12.540
if you intervene on social factors

00:20:12.540 --> 00:20:14.550
that we know are associated with smoking,

00:20:14.550 --> 00:20:17.680
but people who are more food insecure

00:20:17.680 --> 00:20:19.630
are more likely to smoke
and less likely to quit,

00:20:19.630 --> 00:20:21.650
there are studies out
there that show that.

00:20:21.650 --> 00:20:24.090
So if you can intervene on those factors,

00:20:24.090 --> 00:20:26.800
does it change circumstances for them?

00:20:26.800 --> 00:20:30.142
Can we get people out
of the addiction cycle?

00:20:30.142 --> 00:20:33.770
But it's also forced me to work

00:20:33.770 --> 00:20:35.702
in the COVID arena as well.

00:20:35.702 --> 00:20:37.810
I wasn't running, jumping,

00:20:37.810 --> 00:20:41.250
trying to become involved
in reducing disparities

00:20:41.250 --> 00:20:45.227
as it is associated with COVID-19,

00:20:45.227 --> 00:20:47.530
but the communities in which I was working

00:20:47.530 --> 00:20:52.210
were experiencing disparities
in terms of cases,

00:20:52.210 --> 00:20:56.750
in terms of deaths and in
terms of low vaccine rates.

00:20:56.750 --> 00:21:00.790
I mean, some of our counties
still have vaccine rates

00:21:00.790 --> 00:21:04.890
that are in 35%, you know.

00:21:04.890 --> 00:21:07.490
And so we have a long way to go compared

00:21:07.490 --> 00:21:08.853
to Vermont and New York.

00:21:10.170 --> 00:21:11.003
- Really important.

00:21:11.003 --> 00:21:13.420
And just, you know, also just
wanted to acknowledge, again,

00:21:13.420 --> 00:21:16.190
that flexibility in the
fact that you adjusted

00:21:16.190 --> 00:21:17.650
to meet people where they were,

00:21:17.650 --> 00:21:19.740
even though it wasn't your
original research question,

00:21:19.740 --> 00:21:21.250
also knowing that that
takes more work too.

00:21:21.250 --> 00:21:25.168
So I imagine there's a
fine balance there as well,

00:21:25.168 --> 00:21:26.490
and something I'm sure will come back to

00:21:26.490 --> 00:21:27.650
in the conversation.

00:21:27.650 --> 00:21:29.329
But Kathy, I also wanted
to give you a chance

00:21:29.329 --> 00:21:30.162
to respond to that.

00:21:30.162 --> 00:21:31.923
- Well, starting with
your original question,

00:21:31.923 --> 00:21:35.710
you know, I have two, I
guess, personal examples

00:21:35.710 --> 00:21:38.150
of how kind of bringing to life

00:21:38.150 --> 00:21:39.010
what you were just saying.

00:21:39.010 --> 00:21:41.540
But first of all, I need to
say that Mitch is a great boss,

00:21:41.540 --> 00:21:45.580
because he, over the
course of the pandemic,

00:21:45.580 --> 00:21:47.580
he allowed me to do a lot of time off

00:21:47.580 --> 00:21:49.820
from work volunteering.

00:21:49.820 --> 00:21:52.030
Obviously I took leave, but volunteering,

00:21:52.030 --> 00:21:54.993
because you mentioned food insecurity.

00:21:54.993 --> 00:21:58.430
During the pandemic for over a year,

00:21:58.430 --> 00:22:02.913
our church was asked to help
to reach into the community

00:22:02.913 --> 00:22:05.580
to deal specifically with food insecurity.

00:22:05.580 --> 00:22:08.470
And once we started getting
out into the community,

00:22:08.470 --> 00:22:09.710
I mean, it was,

00:22:09.710 --> 00:22:12.790
you know, it was in the
midst of the shutdowns

00:22:12.790 --> 00:22:15.650
and there were no vaccines,

00:22:15.650 --> 00:22:18.570
and we really didn't understand
where COVID was gonna go.

00:22:18.570 --> 00:22:22.450
But the community need was so great.

00:22:22.450 --> 00:22:25.070
And the fear on people's
faces were so great

00:22:25.070 --> 00:22:27.070
that once we started doing it,

00:22:27.070 --> 00:22:29.440
we had more and more and more requests.

00:22:29.440 --> 00:22:33.253
And so basically we would
go out into the community

00:22:33.253 --> 00:22:37.300
every day of the week and
deliver big boxes of food.

00:22:37.300 --> 00:22:38.750
And I would always go on Friday mornings,

00:22:38.750 --> 00:22:41.710
and I would see the same
people over and over again.

00:22:41.710 --> 00:22:44.710
And so many of them deal
with so many of the factors

00:22:44.710 --> 00:22:46.304
that you were talking about.

00:22:46.304 --> 00:22:50.810
But what I would like to say
is that it gave me a belief

00:22:50.810 --> 00:22:54.760
that we can band together
in times of intense trial,

00:22:54.760 --> 00:22:56.980
and we can help others.

00:22:56.980 --> 00:23:01.080
And so I felt encouraged by
that, as sad as it was to see,

00:23:01.080 --> 00:23:04.270
and the other example I would give,

00:23:04.270 --> 00:23:07.313
it was the federal response
to all the children

00:23:07.313 --> 00:23:09.580
that were coming in across the border,

00:23:09.580 --> 00:23:12.540
our southern border without families.

00:23:12.540 --> 00:23:15.520
And so there was a
massive federal outreach

00:23:15.520 --> 00:23:20.364
to go into these cities and
help to bring these children in.

00:23:20.364 --> 00:23:23.209
'Cause once they step foot in the U.S.,

00:23:23.209 --> 00:23:26.500
Health and Human Services
must take care for them.

00:23:26.500 --> 00:23:30.120
And so there was a massive volunteer unit.

00:23:30.120 --> 00:23:32.970
Again, Mitch allowed
me to leave my position

00:23:32.970 --> 00:23:34.240
for six weeks to go.

00:23:34.240 --> 00:23:38.120
And seeing these children coming across,

00:23:38.120 --> 00:23:39.770
it was unbelievable.

00:23:39.770 --> 00:23:41.800
It was sad, but it was also amazing

00:23:41.800 --> 00:23:43.680
to see how the American people,

00:23:43.680 --> 00:23:45.860
the host cities kind of stepped up

00:23:45.860 --> 00:23:47.840
to bringing these children across.

00:23:47.840 --> 00:23:50.930
And in both cases, they
were COVID hot zones, right?

00:23:50.930 --> 00:23:53.240
It's like working in the community,

00:23:53.240 --> 00:23:56.760
tons of COVID and then
bringing these kids in

00:23:56.760 --> 00:23:57.940
and working in the community

00:23:57.940 --> 00:24:00.495
to try to help them find families here.

00:24:00.495 --> 00:24:04.140
But yet it gave me a lot of hope

00:24:04.140 --> 00:24:06.810
that we are a society that
can care about each other.

00:24:06.810 --> 00:24:09.930
And so not necessarily
related to tobacco control,

00:24:09.930 --> 00:24:12.240
but it's certainly, I mean, you know,

00:24:12.240 --> 00:24:14.550
many of these kids came across addicted.

00:24:14.550 --> 00:24:17.790
Many of these kids had
never had, you know,

00:24:17.790 --> 00:24:20.196
even really doctors' appointments.

00:24:20.196 --> 00:24:22.220
And so it was amazing the
care that we gave them,

00:24:22.220 --> 00:24:24.970
and it really encouraged me

00:24:24.970 --> 00:24:27.400
about the good that we can do.

00:24:27.400 --> 00:24:28.787
- Yeah, really well said.

00:24:28.787 --> 00:24:29.822
And I love the way

00:24:29.822 --> 00:24:31.340
that you all have highlighted
just aspects, again,

00:24:31.340 --> 00:24:33.030
of touching on humanity in different ways,

00:24:33.030 --> 00:24:34.680
some within the work, but some, again,

00:24:34.680 --> 00:24:36.690
having that time to be able to step out

00:24:36.690 --> 00:24:39.263
of your official role and to
really be able to meet people

00:24:39.263 --> 00:24:40.710
where they are.

00:24:40.710 --> 00:24:42.820
And Mitch, it sounds like
you've done that a lot of ways,

00:24:42.820 --> 00:24:45.290
even within the CTP organization.

00:24:45.290 --> 00:24:49.040
So again, just, you know, themes
that you all have continued

00:24:49.040 --> 00:24:51.520
to implement in so many important ways.

00:24:51.520 --> 00:24:54.090
I'm also curious how these stories

00:24:54.090 --> 00:24:56.720
have impacted your
motivation going forward.

00:24:56.720 --> 00:24:59.150
I mean, Mitch, you touched
on that with the new normal

00:24:59.150 --> 00:25:00.873
or whatever that might look like.

00:25:00.873 --> 00:25:04.790
But how is this actually
impacting how you approach things

00:25:04.790 --> 00:25:07.543
on a day-to-day basis now
in your current efforts?

00:25:08.500 --> 00:25:11.473
- I think just sort of programmatically,

00:25:12.830 --> 00:25:16.180
it sort of comes back
to some of the notions

00:25:16.180 --> 00:25:17.230
I already touched on.

00:25:19.320 --> 00:25:22.094
Because we're working in an administration

00:25:22.094 --> 00:25:27.094
that has in my entire
career in tobacco control,

00:25:28.160 --> 00:25:31.263
going back almost 30 years,

00:25:32.150 --> 00:25:35.540
I've never seen this kind
of focus on health equity

00:25:35.540 --> 00:25:37.093
and health disparities.

00:25:38.530 --> 00:25:41.250
I think, and this goes way
beyond product regulation.

00:25:41.250 --> 00:25:44.420
This is an unprecedented opportunity

00:25:44.420 --> 00:25:48.180
for comprehensive tobacco
control efforts at all levels,

00:25:48.180 --> 00:25:51.210
federal, state, and local.

00:25:51.210 --> 00:25:56.210
And so it's not directly
related to life in a pandemic.

00:25:57.460 --> 00:25:59.640
It is directly related to everything

00:25:59.640 --> 00:26:04.200
that we are trying to deal with
and struggling as a society

00:26:04.200 --> 00:26:08.970
when it comes to those
who are more well-off

00:26:08.970 --> 00:26:12.200
and less well-off and understanding why.

00:26:12.200 --> 00:26:16.470
Why are entire groups of people

00:26:16.470 --> 00:26:18.853
in this country left behind?

00:26:20.040 --> 00:26:22.440
In our day jobs working on tobacco,

00:26:22.440 --> 00:26:26.404
we know all of the
statistics, they're profound.

00:26:26.404 --> 00:26:30.310
And for me, those population level,

00:26:30.310 --> 00:26:32.810
when we say historically
low levels of adult

00:26:32.810 --> 00:26:34.900
and youth smoking, that's great,

00:26:34.900 --> 00:26:37.450
but who is the remaining smoker?

00:26:37.450 --> 00:26:41.140
And however you slice and dice
it, educational attainment,

00:26:41.140 --> 00:26:44.130
household income, what's
the smoking status

00:26:44.130 --> 00:26:45.446
in the household,

00:26:45.446 --> 00:26:49.060
racial and ethnic groups,
sexual orientation,

00:26:49.060 --> 00:26:50.600
however you slice it,

00:26:50.600 --> 00:26:54.410
there are profound disparities
when it comes to tobacco use.

00:26:54.410 --> 00:26:56.960
And so the opportunity here is finally,

00:26:56.960 --> 00:26:59.460
at the highest levels
attention is being paid.

00:26:59.460 --> 00:27:02.513
And for me, the fundamental
question is being asked, why.

00:27:03.660 --> 00:27:07.880
Even though I think everybody
in the room can answer that,

00:27:07.880 --> 00:27:11.530
that deceptively simple
question's being asked

00:27:11.530 --> 00:27:14.810
in a very, very serious way
on, as far as I'm concerned,

00:27:14.810 --> 00:27:17.060
inside the federal government
at the highest levels

00:27:17.060 --> 00:27:18.063
for the first time.

00:27:19.350 --> 00:27:22.900
And whether it's tobacco
use or food insecurity

00:27:22.900 --> 00:27:27.900
or any other social or public
health issue that we face,

00:27:30.040 --> 00:27:31.680
I'll share Kathy's optimism

00:27:31.680 --> 00:27:36.680
that the sunshine that's
being focused on these issues

00:27:38.820 --> 00:27:41.800
is giving us opportunities
that we have not had before.

00:27:41.800 --> 00:27:43.640
The problems have always been there.

00:27:43.640 --> 00:27:45.300
But this is an unprecedented opportunity

00:27:45.300 --> 00:27:48.063
because of the attention
that they're getting.

00:27:49.289 --> 00:27:51.247
- [Dr. Addy] Really well said.

00:27:51.247 --> 00:27:52.080
Go ahead.

00:27:52.080 --> 00:27:53.520
- I think one thing though,

00:27:53.520 --> 00:27:55.330
and not to take the sad side of it,

00:27:55.330 --> 00:27:58.350
but something that's
become so clear to me,

00:27:58.350 --> 00:28:03.100
especially in our work
with youth is, you know,

00:28:03.100 --> 00:28:05.900
the emerging issues with mental health

00:28:05.900 --> 00:28:09.770
and our area, what we
work most closely with

00:28:09.770 --> 00:28:11.630
is obviously trying to prevent use

00:28:11.630 --> 00:28:12.950
from using tobacco products.

00:28:12.950 --> 00:28:15.930
But we haven't always been successful.

00:28:15.930 --> 00:28:18.380
I mean, there's been a lot of, you know,

00:28:18.380 --> 00:28:20.930
countervailing reasons why
I think a lot of kids were,

00:28:20.930 --> 00:28:25.290
especially started vaping,
and now that they're addicted,

00:28:25.290 --> 00:28:26.730
they reach out to us often

00:28:26.730 --> 00:28:30.760
and with really incredible stories

00:28:30.760 --> 00:28:33.170
about how they never used to be anxious.

00:28:33.170 --> 00:28:34.950
They never used to be afraid.

00:28:34.950 --> 00:28:36.670
Now they feel depressed all the time.

00:28:36.670 --> 00:28:39.340
Now they wanna, they
self-isolate themselves.

00:28:39.340 --> 00:28:44.340
And it really appears that
on top of the pandemic,

00:28:45.620 --> 00:28:49.210
we are seeing many, many
mental health issues emerge.

00:28:49.210 --> 00:28:51.690
And our piece is just one of it.

00:28:51.690 --> 00:28:55.060
And, you know, I always quote this,

00:28:55.060 --> 00:28:56.250
because it was so telling,

00:28:56.250 --> 00:28:59.887
because one teen we were
talking to in the early days

00:28:59.887 --> 00:29:01.850
and talking about the addiction,

00:29:01.850 --> 00:29:03.027
and, you know, he is like,

00:29:03.027 --> 00:29:05.480
"You know, all I did was
just add crap to the crap."

00:29:05.480 --> 00:29:09.087
And I think that there is,
especially if you're addicted,

00:29:09.087 --> 00:29:12.090
you know, as most of you know,

00:29:12.090 --> 00:29:14.090
when you're withdrawing from that nicotine

00:29:14.090 --> 00:29:16.830
and you need another hit,
it's very anxiety producing,

00:29:16.830 --> 00:29:20.684
but when you add that on top of everything

00:29:20.684 --> 00:29:21.989
that was happening with COVID,

00:29:21.989 --> 00:29:24.900
the fact that everybody was
self-isolated in their homes,

00:29:24.900 --> 00:29:25.770
they couldn't go out,

00:29:25.770 --> 00:29:28.830
I think that we have a lot
more mental health issues

00:29:28.830 --> 00:29:30.340
that are emerging

00:29:30.340 --> 00:29:33.330
that are leading to all
sorts of terrible things.

00:29:33.330 --> 00:29:35.230
Other type of coping mechanisms

00:29:35.230 --> 00:29:37.100
with different types of drugs,

00:29:37.100 --> 00:29:39.210
suicide rates seem incredibly high.

00:29:39.210 --> 00:29:41.810
So I think, you know, to me,

00:29:41.810 --> 00:29:44.570
it's made me realize that
there's just so much more

00:29:44.570 --> 00:29:46.630
that needs to be done.

00:29:46.630 --> 00:29:49.060
And for me, mental health
has become a real big,

00:29:49.060 --> 00:29:52.330
emerging issue that we're
developing messaging on now.

00:29:52.330 --> 00:29:53.177
- Yeah, really important.

00:29:53.177 --> 00:29:55.280
And I'm glad to hear that
message is being developed,

00:29:55.280 --> 00:29:58.360
but also seems like the
conversation is being elevated

00:29:58.360 --> 00:30:00.977
in ways that it wasn't before.

00:30:00.977 --> 00:30:03.150
And especially, even as
the data's coming out

00:30:03.150 --> 00:30:06.350
with all the stressors that
have been added with COVID.

00:30:06.350 --> 00:30:08.840
Dr. Fagan, I also wonder if
you had a response to that,

00:30:08.840 --> 00:30:10.610
just based in the communities
that you've worked in,

00:30:10.610 --> 00:30:14.100
where obviously stressors
can lead to a lot

00:30:14.100 --> 00:30:14.990
of these different components,

00:30:14.990 --> 00:30:17.070
both from the mental
health side and youth,

00:30:17.070 --> 00:30:17.983
from youth side,

00:30:19.016 --> 00:30:20.200
and how you've continued
to trying to approach that,

00:30:20.200 --> 00:30:21.690
even though there's a lot of work.

00:30:21.690 --> 00:30:22.936
- Yeah.

00:30:22.936 --> 00:30:26.120
I think the COVID-19 and other things

00:30:26.120 --> 00:30:27.880
that are happening in
the world are forcing us

00:30:27.880 --> 00:30:30.073
to think about workforce issues.

00:30:31.080 --> 00:30:36.080
And how do we retain viable
workforce in all sectors?

00:30:38.330 --> 00:30:42.770
How do we continue to build
the scientific workforce,

00:30:42.770 --> 00:30:46.620
the practitioner workforce,
and the advocacy workforce?

00:30:46.620 --> 00:30:48.363
Those are real issues.

00:30:52.115 --> 00:30:54.533
And also create new work climates

00:30:57.090 --> 00:31:00.290
for people to live, breathe, and work in

00:31:00.290 --> 00:31:05.290
that are more humane and
still get at productivity.

00:31:05.490 --> 00:31:09.860
And so my experience during
COVID-19 is, you know,

00:31:09.860 --> 00:31:14.860
I went home, I took my
desktop home and it was great.

00:31:15.350 --> 00:31:16.950
And I created more work for myself,

00:31:16.950 --> 00:31:19.830
because not just because of COVID-19,

00:31:19.830 --> 00:31:21.840
but you didn't have to run to meetings.

00:31:21.840 --> 00:31:24.600
I didn't have to travel to work.

00:31:24.600 --> 00:31:26.100
I saved on that time.

00:31:26.100 --> 00:31:28.060
So I could stop at lunchtime, go walk,

00:31:28.060 --> 00:31:30.370
come back and still be productive.

00:31:30.370 --> 00:31:33.530
Now transitioning back into the workforce,

00:31:33.530 --> 00:31:37.070
and you've created an
extra four hours of work,

00:31:37.070 --> 00:31:39.940
but now you still have your four hour,

00:31:39.940 --> 00:31:42.790
getting ready for work,
coming home added on.

00:31:42.790 --> 00:31:45.550
How do we make those adjustments?

00:31:45.550 --> 00:31:48.904
And people like Mitch
are retiring. (laughs)

00:31:48.904 --> 00:31:51.857
That's his adjustment.
- That's a stressor.

00:31:51.857 --> 00:31:53.160
That's a stressor.

00:31:53.160 --> 00:31:54.150
- Look, we said we're gonna try

00:31:54.150 --> 00:31:56.104
to make people laugh a little.

00:31:56.104 --> 00:31:56.958
There it is.

00:31:56.958 --> 00:32:00.680
So there was a joke, (laughs)
but those are real issues.

00:32:00.680 --> 00:32:03.490
And it's a real mental
health issue as well

00:32:03.490 --> 00:32:05.441
trying to make those adjustments

00:32:05.441 --> 00:32:07.920
when there's such a great need.

00:32:07.920 --> 00:32:09.460
But at the same time,

00:32:09.460 --> 00:32:11.800
we have to have strike a life balance.

00:32:11.800 --> 00:32:14.540
'Cause I'm getting away from
saying work-life balance,

00:32:14.540 --> 00:32:15.620
it's life balance.

00:32:15.620 --> 00:32:16.453
That's what it is.

00:32:16.453 --> 00:32:19.700
If you lead with the work
part, we get it wrong, right.

00:32:19.700 --> 00:32:21.000
It's life balance.

00:32:21.000 --> 00:32:24.210
And how do we make that happen?

00:32:24.210 --> 00:32:26.610
We haven't had enough conversations

00:32:26.610 --> 00:32:29.040
about our transitioning back

00:32:29.040 --> 00:32:33.040
to whatever that normal looks like,

00:32:33.040 --> 00:32:35.494
'cause it's not what it was yesterday.

00:32:35.494 --> 00:32:38.420
People kept saying, get back to normal.

00:32:38.420 --> 00:32:41.500
I don't know what that means right now,

00:32:41.500 --> 00:32:45.070
but we need to have strategic discussions

00:32:45.070 --> 00:32:47.550
about what that looks like.

00:32:47.550 --> 00:32:51.420
And I think we haven't
done enough of that.

00:32:51.420 --> 00:32:56.420
We have to reframe what a
research field looks like now.

00:32:57.420 --> 00:33:00.220
What does an academic workplace look like?

00:33:00.220 --> 00:33:03.000
What does a federal workplace look like

00:33:03.000 --> 00:33:07.230
that is healthy, mentally healthy, viable,

00:33:07.230 --> 00:33:12.230
and productive and not
contributing to premature deaths?

00:33:13.560 --> 00:33:15.460
- It's been really interesting to see,

00:33:16.540 --> 00:33:18.730
just inside of our agency,

00:33:18.730 --> 00:33:21.420
and I can say this goes beyond our center,

00:33:21.420 --> 00:33:23.050
because as a center director,

00:33:23.050 --> 00:33:25.545
I hear from leadership throughout FDA,

00:33:25.545 --> 00:33:26.923
and FDA is 18,000 people,

00:33:28.000 --> 00:33:33.000
that the adaptation to 100%
remote or 100% telework,

00:33:33.660 --> 00:33:35.580
once we got through
some technical problems

00:33:35.580 --> 00:33:39.200
literally just during the
first week was pretty smooth.

00:33:39.200 --> 00:33:42.280
And to Pebbles' point,
people got used to it

00:33:42.280 --> 00:33:45.190
and actually became more productive

00:33:45.190 --> 00:33:47.120
to the point where that raised issues

00:33:47.120 --> 00:33:50.990
of actually work being piled on,

00:33:50.990 --> 00:33:52.440
because they didn't
have to do the commuting

00:33:52.440 --> 00:33:54.820
and they didn't have all the other things

00:33:54.820 --> 00:33:55.653
that come with that.

00:33:55.653 --> 00:34:00.624
And so where FDA as an
organization finds itself now is

00:34:00.624 --> 00:34:04.010
asking some really profound questions

00:34:04.010 --> 00:34:08.610
about its physical
footprint going forward.

00:34:08.610 --> 00:34:13.610
Does an agency like FDA
need all of those buildings,

00:34:15.010 --> 00:34:17.530
or in whatever the new normal is,

00:34:17.530 --> 00:34:19.100
is it gonna be, you know,

00:34:19.100 --> 00:34:24.100
so different that rethinking
the need for physical workspace

00:34:24.980 --> 00:34:27.630
or how much of it is something

00:34:27.630 --> 00:34:29.780
that is starting to be discussed?

00:34:29.780 --> 00:34:31.990
And it will be very,
very interesting to see,

00:34:31.990 --> 00:34:34.470
and assuming those
conversations are happening

00:34:34.470 --> 00:34:37.436
in other small, medium and
large size organizations.

00:34:37.436 --> 00:34:39.690
I don't think we can predict

00:34:39.690 --> 00:34:40.760
what the future's gonna look like,

00:34:40.760 --> 00:34:42.620
other than it's gonna
be very, very different

00:34:42.620 --> 00:34:44.350
from what it looked like for us

00:34:44.350 --> 00:34:45.860
when it comes to workplace issues

00:34:45.860 --> 00:34:48.860
that Pebbles was talking about
when it all settles down.

00:34:48.860 --> 00:34:50.710
I think it's gonna be really different.

00:34:50.710 --> 00:34:55.210
And I think, I hope a much better balance

00:34:55.210 --> 00:34:57.630
between work responsibilities,

00:34:57.630 --> 00:35:01.560
making it easier on the
people who are doing the work

00:35:01.560 --> 00:35:04.473
while balancing familial
responsibilities as well.

00:35:06.000 --> 00:35:06.833
- And I think that's really important

00:35:06.833 --> 00:35:09.520
that you both highlighted
that there is a danger

00:35:09.520 --> 00:35:12.192
to try to go back to
what was normal before

00:35:12.192 --> 00:35:15.800
in the sense that we don't
wanna lose everything

00:35:15.800 --> 00:35:18.150
that we've learned over
the last couple years,

00:35:18.150 --> 00:35:19.580
things that have been ignored before

00:35:19.580 --> 00:35:21.450
that now have more attention.

00:35:21.450 --> 00:35:25.810
And so that in itself, I think,
is even attention for some,

00:35:25.810 --> 00:35:27.720
because there can be this tendency to try

00:35:27.720 --> 00:35:30.010
and want to return to what was normal

00:35:30.010 --> 00:35:34.370
with all of the caveats
that come with that as well.

00:35:34.370 --> 00:35:36.550
So maybe more so for Kathy and Dr. Fagan,

00:35:36.550 --> 00:35:39.240
I'm curious from your standpoints,

00:35:39.240 --> 00:35:41.900
just in the work that you do
and in the communication space,

00:35:41.900 --> 00:35:44.620
how you think it is important for us

00:35:44.620 --> 00:35:46.030
to continue to move things forward.

00:35:46.030 --> 00:35:49.200
So this doesn't become
passing, in a sense,

00:35:49.200 --> 00:35:50.610
'cause there is, I do often sense

00:35:50.610 --> 00:35:51.980
that pull for people to,

00:35:51.980 --> 00:35:54.240
there're trying to go back
to what felt familiar,

00:35:54.240 --> 00:35:55.950
even though there were a
lot of problematic aspects

00:35:55.950 --> 00:35:57.700
with what was familiar.

00:35:57.700 --> 00:35:59.950
So how does that come up, I guess,

00:35:59.950 --> 00:36:01.937
in a day-to-day basis for each of you?

00:36:02.889 --> 00:36:05.900
- Well, I think especially
for the office that I oversee,

00:36:05.900 --> 00:36:10.650
I think people have
learned to love the ability

00:36:10.650 --> 00:36:12.240
to work exclusively from home.

00:36:12.240 --> 00:36:15.560
And so if anything, I think, you know,

00:36:15.560 --> 00:36:17.640
what I'm hopeful about is the opportunity

00:36:17.640 --> 00:36:21.140
to explore what a hybrid
workspace might look like.

00:36:21.140 --> 00:36:23.530
And you know, maybe that means

00:36:23.530 --> 00:36:26.660
that for really important
times of collaboration,

00:36:26.660 --> 00:36:28.120
people are able to come together.

00:36:28.120 --> 00:36:30.280
But you know, I think that we've learned

00:36:30.280 --> 00:36:33.607
that people can be, can do
their job and do it really well

00:36:33.607 --> 00:36:36.400
and not be in a physical environment

00:36:36.400 --> 00:36:37.280
where you're all together.

00:36:37.280 --> 00:36:40.290
So for me, I think the
challenge is gonna be finding

00:36:40.290 --> 00:36:42.630
that right mix, because
right now everybody has,

00:36:42.630 --> 00:36:45.910
I guess what I would call,
somewhat of a taste of freedom.

00:36:45.910 --> 00:36:48.480
And we really haven't missed a beat,

00:36:48.480 --> 00:36:49.740
as Mitch said multiple times,

00:36:49.740 --> 00:36:53.000
in terms of productivity and
being able to do our jobs.

00:36:53.000 --> 00:36:57.840
It's like, our bread and butter is talking

00:36:57.840 --> 00:37:00.790
to the audiences that we're
trying to develop messaging for.

00:37:00.790 --> 00:37:04.363
And in the early days we had
to really like figure out

00:37:04.363 --> 00:37:05.196
how we're gonna do that, but
now we've figured it out.

00:37:05.196 --> 00:37:07.090
It's like it's all done online.

00:37:07.090 --> 00:37:10.320
And we really haven't had an interruption

00:37:10.320 --> 00:37:15.320
in our ability to actually
deeply have a good conversation

00:37:15.430 --> 00:37:16.970
with a youth or an adult.

00:37:16.970 --> 00:37:20.387
And so we don't need to go
back to traveling to 28 cities

00:37:20.387 --> 00:37:22.750
for 48 focus groups.

00:37:22.750 --> 00:37:23.860
We know we can do it online.

00:37:23.860 --> 00:37:25.910
And so I think we've learned to adapt,

00:37:25.910 --> 00:37:27.460
and I think we're super productive.

00:37:27.460 --> 00:37:29.062
And I think people love the opportunity

00:37:29.062 --> 00:37:31.170
to only dress from here up

00:37:31.170 --> 00:37:33.450
and not worry about what's down below

00:37:33.450 --> 00:37:36.070
and walk into the kitchen and make lunch

00:37:36.070 --> 00:37:38.530
or walk and have a nice afternoon walk.

00:37:38.530 --> 00:37:42.810
And so I think for me, it's
trying to find the right balance

00:37:42.810 --> 00:37:46.430
where there's the opportunity,
'cause I also worry about,

00:37:46.430 --> 00:37:48.450
especially those who are younger,

00:37:48.450 --> 00:37:50.370
who really would benefit
from the opportunity

00:37:50.370 --> 00:37:53.190
to listen in meetings,
see how things happen,

00:37:53.190 --> 00:37:55.770
see how like creative brainstormings go.

00:37:55.770 --> 00:37:58.725
And I do worry that some
of the younger generation,

00:37:58.725 --> 00:38:03.725
if they're not able to be in
person when lightning strikes,

00:38:03.779 --> 00:38:06.960
I do think something is lost over Zoom.

00:38:06.960 --> 00:38:09.640
And so my worries are,

00:38:09.640 --> 00:38:11.615
how do you keep an engaged workforce

00:38:11.615 --> 00:38:13.550
such that they still feel

00:38:13.550 --> 00:38:15.270
like they have balance in their life,

00:38:15.270 --> 00:38:17.483
but that you can continue
to grow and thrive

00:38:17.483 --> 00:38:20.723
in somewhat of a shared
space, but not all the time?

00:38:22.118 --> 00:38:24.558
- Now I'm going juggling.

00:38:24.558 --> 00:38:28.740
- I mean, I would love to see
more strategic discussions

00:38:28.740 --> 00:38:31.144
about what the new workplace looks like.

00:38:31.144 --> 00:38:33.723
I have talked with my team,

00:38:33.723 --> 00:38:37.680
they're all here at the meeting with me,

00:38:37.680 --> 00:38:42.680
and for career development
to give them a break as well,

00:38:43.330 --> 00:38:47.170
come to Baltimore, you
know, see what we do here.

00:38:47.170 --> 00:38:50.640
But we've had discussions about,

00:38:50.640 --> 00:38:53.790
how do we move to a
four-day work week now?

00:38:53.790 --> 00:38:56.173
That's a tough thing to figure out,

00:38:56.173 --> 00:38:57.750
places like Finland and other
countries have it figured out.

00:38:57.750 --> 00:39:01.660
And the research is fairly solid
on the fact that, you know,

00:39:01.660 --> 00:39:04.690
those countries that have
moved to that particular model,

00:39:04.690 --> 00:39:07.816
that the people are as
productive or more productive.

00:39:07.816 --> 00:39:11.730
And so we have to think
very carefully about that.

00:39:11.730 --> 00:39:13.370
You know, the US is very slow

00:39:13.370 --> 00:39:15.640
to adopt some of these models

00:39:15.640 --> 00:39:18.430
that work in other places
and these countries,

00:39:18.430 --> 00:39:20.750
other countries are
doing just fine. (laughs)

00:39:20.750 --> 00:39:22.640
They're doing just fine.

00:39:22.640 --> 00:39:26.460
So we have to reevaluate our value system

00:39:26.460 --> 00:39:28.683
around life itself.

00:39:29.680 --> 00:39:31.650
And I don't think reevaluating,

00:39:31.650 --> 00:39:33.980
it means that we're giving up something

00:39:33.980 --> 00:39:35.840
in terms of making progress,

00:39:35.840 --> 00:39:39.490
because the reevaluation
may help us get closer

00:39:39.490 --> 00:39:43.540
to progress faster and expeditiously,

00:39:43.540 --> 00:39:48.110
as compared to the way that
we have been doing things.

00:39:48.110 --> 00:39:49.530
So when I said, you know,

00:39:49.530 --> 00:39:51.950
we have to look at these tragedies

00:39:51.950 --> 00:39:54.030
as opportunities sometimes,

00:39:54.030 --> 00:39:55.560
because if you, you know,

00:39:55.560 --> 00:39:57.090
the tragedy is the tragedy.

00:39:57.090 --> 00:40:02.090
And if you let it take you
down with mental health issues,

00:40:02.340 --> 00:40:05.210
you'll destroy yourself.

00:40:05.210 --> 00:40:08.540
So you have to start looking
at things as opportunities

00:40:08.540 --> 00:40:12.997
and what can we do
strategically around the nation

00:40:12.997 --> 00:40:14.540
to begin to plan,

00:40:14.540 --> 00:40:19.200
and I think the people in this
room are primed to do that,

00:40:19.200 --> 00:40:23.272
around changing work climate, you know,

00:40:23.272 --> 00:40:26.126
eliminating things such
as microaggression,

00:40:26.126 --> 00:40:28.640
eliminating things such as biases,

00:40:28.640 --> 00:40:30.870
eliminating things such
as structural racism,

00:40:30.870 --> 00:40:34.045
the academic system,
the federal government,

00:40:34.045 --> 00:40:36.088
doing things like making sure

00:40:36.088 --> 00:40:39.740
that women who are
concentrated in particular jobs

00:40:39.740 --> 00:40:42.400
are paid fair wages and making sure

00:40:42.400 --> 00:40:45.550
that women who do community-based work,

00:40:45.550 --> 00:40:48.610
who are out there busting their butts,

00:40:48.610 --> 00:40:51.440
get paid the same wages of the person

00:40:51.440 --> 00:40:53.090
who's working in the laboratory.

00:40:53.090 --> 00:40:55.750
I mean, we have to think differently

00:40:55.750 --> 00:40:58.210
about how we're gonna move forward,

00:40:58.210 --> 00:41:03.210
but it takes willingness to
engage in strategic discussions

00:41:03.780 --> 00:41:08.780
around that in the same way
that private industry has done,

00:41:09.220 --> 00:41:11.600
and say, "Hey, what are
we gonna do differently?"

00:41:11.600 --> 00:41:13.720
Let's look at the workplace.

00:41:13.720 --> 00:41:16.110
Let's look at the goals that
we're trying to accomplish

00:41:16.110 --> 00:41:18.140
in tobacco prevention and control.

00:41:18.140 --> 00:41:20.466
How do we get there, and how
do we shape the workplace

00:41:20.466 --> 00:41:25.466
to get to that goal
without killing ourselves

00:41:27.930 --> 00:41:31.840
and without contributing
to the premature morbidity

00:41:31.840 --> 00:41:36.080
and mortality by killing
the workforce off?

00:41:36.080 --> 00:41:39.080
So I mean, those are real issues.

00:41:39.080 --> 00:41:40.060
- Yeah. I definitely agree.

00:41:40.060 --> 00:41:42.720
I mean, there's so many
great things to unpack there.

00:41:42.720 --> 00:41:44.210
To Kathy's point from before though.

00:41:44.210 --> 00:41:46.030
I mean, just in a humorous way,

00:41:46.030 --> 00:41:47.670
the only dressing from the top,

00:41:47.670 --> 00:41:49.590
if you all will notice that we decided

00:41:49.590 --> 00:41:51.740
and made a pact that we were
gonna do the full dress today.

00:41:51.740 --> 00:41:54.260
So no one came pajama bottoms,

00:41:54.260 --> 00:41:56.080
even though I'm sure
that was a temptation,

00:41:56.080 --> 00:41:58.340
just how things have
been going in the past.

00:41:58.340 --> 00:42:02.580
But Dr. Fagan, so many key
things, which you talked about.

00:42:02.580 --> 00:42:04.400
One, I mean, not to complicate it,

00:42:04.400 --> 00:42:06.470
but yes, to complicate it in a sense too,

00:42:06.470 --> 00:42:08.690
as you've mentioned in other settings,

00:42:08.690 --> 00:42:10.670
those who are doing health
disparities research,

00:42:10.670 --> 00:42:13.740
who are often spread thin in the place.

00:42:13.740 --> 00:42:16.360
And so that was and has been the norm.

00:42:16.360 --> 00:42:19.161
Now you add on this layer
of also trying to adjust

00:42:19.161 --> 00:42:21.930
and make sure that there is balance

00:42:21.930 --> 00:42:24.440
in the workday and workforce.

00:42:24.440 --> 00:42:25.667
How do you, and you've
started to talk about it,

00:42:25.667 --> 00:42:28.140
but how do you put those together?

00:42:28.140 --> 00:42:29.960
I mean, I guess the strategic
piece is part of that,

00:42:29.960 --> 00:42:32.060
but there may not be an easy answer,

00:42:32.060 --> 00:42:33.570
but I just wanted to at
least kind of highlight that.

00:42:33.570 --> 00:42:35.218
'Cause I know it's something.

00:42:35.218 --> 00:42:36.201
- Oh, my goodness.

00:42:36.201 --> 00:42:37.390
Sade and I just had this conversation

00:42:37.390 --> 00:42:38.803
about two hours ago.

00:42:39.800 --> 00:42:43.530
Sometimes, you know, I think it's helpful

00:42:43.530 --> 00:42:46.890
for us to vocalize some honest things,

00:42:46.890 --> 00:42:49.220
which is sometimes you
just have to check out

00:42:50.383 --> 00:42:54.290
and take a break. (laughs)

00:42:54.290 --> 00:42:56.880
And if that means you may not respond

00:42:56.880 --> 00:43:01.130
to an email ever, it doesn't mean

00:43:01.130 --> 00:43:02.830
that you don't wanna respond to it,

00:43:02.830 --> 00:43:05.830
but sometimes you gotta save yourself.

00:43:05.830 --> 00:43:07.703
Sometimes it means,

00:43:08.580 --> 00:43:12.033
and I'll give this
example, two Fridays ago,

00:43:14.396 --> 00:43:17.800
my team had a tough day

00:43:17.800 --> 00:43:21.700
and I wanted to take them out

00:43:21.700 --> 00:43:23.020
so that we could, you know,

00:43:23.020 --> 00:43:25.080
we were supposed to do a luncheon

00:43:25.080 --> 00:43:28.110
for celebrating employee day.

00:43:28.110 --> 00:43:31.440
And I said, "Oh, this
afternoon, let's go out."

00:43:31.440 --> 00:43:33.657
They were like, "Nope, no way.

00:43:33.657 --> 00:43:36.210
"We need a break today."

00:43:36.210 --> 00:43:38.543
And you have to honor that.

00:43:39.433 --> 00:43:41.340
And so sometimes you have to check out,

00:43:41.340 --> 00:43:44.623
sometimes you have to schedule breaks,

00:43:45.480 --> 00:43:46.650
you have to do it for yourself

00:43:46.650 --> 00:43:50.200
and for the people who are
working on your projects.

00:43:50.200 --> 00:43:53.420
Sometimes you have to allow that,

00:43:53.420 --> 00:43:56.470
sometimes you have to
facilitate that as well.

00:43:56.470 --> 00:44:01.470
Sometimes you have to say,
"Today, everybody leave.

00:44:02.357 --> 00:44:04.360
"We're done today."

00:44:04.360 --> 00:44:06.967
Sometimes they tell
me, "We're done today."

00:44:08.740 --> 00:44:11.970
And when they say that,
I'm okay with that.

00:44:11.970 --> 00:44:16.840
So you have to, you know,
even as a manager of people,

00:44:16.840 --> 00:44:19.230
you have to be attentive to folks' needs.

00:44:19.230 --> 00:44:21.620
Sometimes you're trying to save yourself,

00:44:21.620 --> 00:44:23.410
but you also have to make sure

00:44:23.410 --> 00:44:25.810
that you're helping them as well

00:44:25.810 --> 00:44:28.080
in this complicated environment.

00:44:28.080 --> 00:44:30.380
But doing health disparities
research, I mean,

00:44:31.610 --> 00:44:35.253
those of us who have been
doing it for decades,

00:44:37.440 --> 00:44:40.150
I say bear the burden
of doing the research

00:44:40.150 --> 00:44:42.490
and people who are new to it

00:44:44.350 --> 00:44:46.800
may not hang in there as long,

00:44:46.800 --> 00:44:49.450
because you really have to be resilient.

00:44:49.450 --> 00:44:52.380
There's a high level, not a low level,

00:44:52.380 --> 00:44:54.350
very high level of resiliency

00:44:54.350 --> 00:44:57.840
that you have to have
in order to survive it,

00:44:57.840 --> 00:44:59.170
because the need is so great.

00:44:59.170 --> 00:45:01.170
Just like I mentioned earlier, you know,

00:45:01.170 --> 00:45:03.120
I've been working in tobacco disparities.

00:45:03.120 --> 00:45:07.010
I also do cancer disparities
with colorectal cancer,

00:45:07.010 --> 00:45:08.903
and then COVID got added on.

00:45:10.110 --> 00:45:13.060
And so, it's a lot.
- That's a lot.

00:45:13.060 --> 00:45:15.053
- But there's a great need out there.

00:45:16.920 --> 00:45:21.920
But what brings joy to that is to see

00:45:23.400 --> 00:45:26.440
that you are making a difference,

00:45:26.440 --> 00:45:30.080
that you're changing someone's life.

00:45:30.080 --> 00:45:33.760
And I don't mean looking
at the survey data.

00:45:33.760 --> 00:45:35.620
I'm talking about
looking at people's faces

00:45:35.620 --> 00:45:37.750
and talking to them on the phone,

00:45:37.750 --> 00:45:40.783
and my team coming back to me saying hey,

00:45:40.783 --> 00:45:45.570
such-and-such really
appreciated what we did.

00:45:45.570 --> 00:45:47.530
That makes the difference,

00:45:47.530 --> 00:45:49.280
and that keeps you going every day.

00:45:51.020 --> 00:45:53.380
- Yeah, really important and
grounding in a lot of ways

00:45:53.380 --> 00:45:56.950
as well, despite all the
effort and resilience

00:45:56.950 --> 00:45:59.481
that has to be there to maintain.

00:45:59.481 --> 00:46:00.800
So in a lot of ways, I think, again,

00:46:00.800 --> 00:46:02.300
just giving honor where honor is due

00:46:02.300 --> 00:46:04.640
for someone like you who
has been so deeply engaged

00:46:04.640 --> 00:46:05.530
in this work for so long

00:46:05.530 --> 00:46:08.330
and knowing it's making that
impact is very encouraging.

00:46:09.200 --> 00:46:10.400
So I know it's not easy.

00:46:12.610 --> 00:46:15.700
Mitch, to pivot back to
you as well on that note,

00:46:15.700 --> 00:46:20.140
as someone who is shifting
into retirement, just again,

00:46:20.140 --> 00:46:23.260
curious to hear a little
bit about what motivated you

00:46:23.260 --> 00:46:25.340
to get into the work in the first place

00:46:26.323 --> 00:46:30.840
and what your, I guess your
greatest point of a achievement

00:46:30.840 --> 00:46:33.381
and gratification has been

00:46:33.381 --> 00:46:36.233
with this little long
and an impactful career.

00:46:37.410 --> 00:46:41.210
- Well, other than knowing
that I wanted to be a lawyer

00:46:41.210 --> 00:46:42.980
and did not wanna work for a law firm

00:46:42.980 --> 00:46:44.260
and did not wanna work for a corporation,

00:46:44.260 --> 00:46:46.850
I just wanted to do public interest law,

00:46:46.850 --> 00:46:50.023
my career has been
completely serendipitous.

00:46:51.240 --> 00:46:53.120
And it all started with the first job

00:46:53.120 --> 00:46:54.905
that I had out of law school

00:46:54.905 --> 00:46:56.760
that you mentioned working for the Center

00:46:56.760 --> 00:46:58.740
for Science in the Public Interest.

00:46:58.740 --> 00:47:00.580
But honestly, if my first
job outta law school

00:47:00.580 --> 00:47:02.880
had been working for a
civil rights organization

00:47:02.880 --> 00:47:06.780
or an environmental group
or a prisoner rights group,

00:47:06.780 --> 00:47:09.570
I think my career path would've
been completely different,

00:47:09.570 --> 00:47:11.660
but that first job was
working on food labeling,

00:47:11.660 --> 00:47:14.300
food safety and nutrition for six years.

00:47:14.300 --> 00:47:17.190
And I did litigation, I did lobbying,

00:47:17.190 --> 00:47:19.240
I did a lot of media work,

00:47:19.240 --> 00:47:21.420
and a lot of interactions with FDA.

00:47:21.420 --> 00:47:25.200
And it's this summer
would've been 40 years

00:47:25.200 --> 00:47:27.800
of my working on FDA-related issues,

00:47:27.800 --> 00:47:31.830
but it was really happenstance

00:47:31.830 --> 00:47:33.880
once I was kind of put on that path

00:47:33.880 --> 00:47:35.560
and realized the work

00:47:35.560 --> 00:47:36.890
that the Food and Drug
Administration does,

00:47:36.890 --> 00:47:41.490
whether I'm there trying to advance policy

00:47:41.490 --> 00:47:44.040
or on the outside trying
to influence policy

00:47:45.181 --> 00:47:47.540
was something that I
was very interested in.

00:47:47.540 --> 00:47:51.810
The tobacco work for me started in 1994,

00:47:51.810 --> 00:47:54.610
because the commissioner at
the time David Kessler said,

00:47:55.466 --> 00:47:57.929
"We need to investigate
the tobacco industry.

00:47:57.929 --> 00:48:01.017
"We need to truly
understand what they know

00:48:01.017 --> 00:48:02.947
"about the role of nicotine in the design

00:48:02.947 --> 00:48:04.727
"and manufacture of cigarettes

00:48:04.727 --> 00:48:05.940
"and smokeless tobacco products."

00:48:05.940 --> 00:48:07.900
E-cigarettes didn't exist then.

00:48:07.900 --> 00:48:09.910
And smokeless tobacco was the poster child

00:48:09.910 --> 00:48:12.963
for harm reduction back in the 1990s.

00:48:14.100 --> 00:48:19.100
And it just all happened after that.

00:48:19.165 --> 00:48:21.320
And it was Dr. Kessler who
gave me the opportunity

00:48:21.320 --> 00:48:23.020
to work on tobacco.

00:48:23.020 --> 00:48:25.483
And as you all know, if you're here,

00:48:26.740 --> 00:48:28.930
you start working on this,
whatever your day job is,

00:48:28.930 --> 00:48:32.320
and there's kind of a
different notion of addiction

00:48:32.320 --> 00:48:33.293
to the issues.

00:48:34.682 --> 00:48:37.561
I'm retiring, but the last 28 years

00:48:37.561 --> 00:48:40.620
of my FDA-related career,

00:48:40.620 --> 00:48:42.980
it's been a labor of love
with all of the challenges

00:48:42.980 --> 00:48:45.130
and with all the ups and downs,

00:48:45.130 --> 00:48:47.270
it's been an absolute labor of love,

00:48:47.270 --> 00:48:48.930
and I couldn't have planned it.

00:48:48.930 --> 00:48:52.240
Like I said, it really was serendipitous

00:48:52.240 --> 00:48:57.060
from that very first job right
after I graduated law school.

00:48:57.060 --> 00:48:58.570
- I know a lot of people are thankful

00:48:58.570 --> 00:48:59.810
for that serendipity

00:48:59.810 --> 00:49:01.770
for the effort that you've
put in over the years.

00:49:01.770 --> 00:49:04.850
So I appreciate you sharing that as well.

00:49:04.850 --> 00:49:06.130
As we're getting close to our time,

00:49:06.130 --> 00:49:08.370
I did wanna pivot to some
of the public engagement

00:49:08.370 --> 00:49:10.170
a bit as well.

00:49:10.170 --> 00:49:12.960
Obviously, you know, Kathy,
that's your official job,

00:49:12.960 --> 00:49:14.130
but in a lot of ways,

00:49:14.130 --> 00:49:16.510
a lot of all of you are
involved in public engagement

00:49:16.510 --> 00:49:17.870
in some shape or form.

00:49:17.870 --> 00:49:20.700
But Kathy, just to pivot to you again,

00:49:20.700 --> 00:49:23.490
just to talk a little bit
about that within this realm

00:49:23.490 --> 00:49:24.323
of the disparities,

00:49:24.323 --> 00:49:26.430
'cause I know that's been a big point

00:49:26.430 --> 00:49:28.620
of emphasis and intentional.

00:49:28.620 --> 00:49:30.700
So can you talk a little bit
about how that came about

00:49:30.700 --> 00:49:32.730
and what you've tried
to do within that space,

00:49:32.730 --> 00:49:34.590
even as you're trying
to reach broad audiences

00:49:34.590 --> 00:49:35.460
at the same time?

00:49:35.460 --> 00:49:36.293
- Sure.

00:49:36.293 --> 00:49:38.900
So no matter what the issue is,

00:49:38.900 --> 00:49:41.890
and you mentioned my
time at the Ad Council,

00:49:41.890 --> 00:49:44.640
we oversaw almost 30
different pieces of business

00:49:44.640 --> 00:49:46.419
for federal agencies.

00:49:46.419 --> 00:49:50.510
But what was the tie that
bound all of them together

00:49:50.510 --> 00:49:53.940
was that, you know, we were
trying to help people understand

00:49:53.940 --> 00:49:55.540
whatever the issue was

00:49:55.540 --> 00:50:00.540
so that they could potentially
lead healthier, safer,

00:50:00.630 --> 00:50:03.020
more empowered lives.

00:50:03.020 --> 00:50:06.240
And so, you know, for me,
whatever the issue is,

00:50:06.240 --> 00:50:10.450
you have to understand which
audiences would most benefit

00:50:10.450 --> 00:50:11.500
from whatever the issue is

00:50:11.500 --> 00:50:15.420
or which audiences are most
disproportionately hurt

00:50:15.420 --> 00:50:16.640
by whatever the issue is.

00:50:16.640 --> 00:50:21.020
And then, my suggestion is that we don't,

00:50:21.020 --> 00:50:23.330
you don't treat the audience
as they're homogeneous.

00:50:23.330 --> 00:50:27.140
You really have to understand,
like in the case of youth,

00:50:27.140 --> 00:50:29.310
who were the youth that
were smoking cigarettes.

00:50:29.310 --> 00:50:31.930
And when you really peel
back the layer of the onion

00:50:31.930 --> 00:50:33.900
through comprehensive research,

00:50:33.900 --> 00:50:37.090
you understand that a picture emerges

00:50:37.090 --> 00:50:38.800
of those who are most at risk.

00:50:38.800 --> 00:50:41.280
And then you have to understand why,

00:50:41.280 --> 00:50:46.280
what made American Indian,
Alaska Native teens so at risk?

00:50:46.810 --> 00:50:49.950
What makes African
American youth so at risk

00:50:49.950 --> 00:50:50.790
for this product?

00:50:50.790 --> 00:50:54.790
And if you treat them as individuals,

00:50:54.790 --> 00:50:58.480
not as a mass population
and you peel back the layer

00:50:58.480 --> 00:51:01.470
of the onion to understand what
their current knowledge is,

00:51:01.470 --> 00:51:03.860
what their attitudes,
what their beliefs are,

00:51:03.860 --> 00:51:06.470
what their barriers
are that are, you know,

00:51:06.470 --> 00:51:08.120
making them do the behavior

00:51:08.120 --> 00:51:10.900
or conversely making
them stop the behavior,

00:51:10.900 --> 00:51:12.990
you can begin to develop messaging

00:51:12.990 --> 00:51:15.700
that will be relevant and persuasive,

00:51:15.700 --> 00:51:18.530
but based on the uniqueness
of that individual.

00:51:18.530 --> 00:51:23.370
And so to me, it's
about what the issue is,

00:51:23.370 --> 00:51:24.939
who is most hurt by the issue,

00:51:24.939 --> 00:51:26.814
what can you learn about them,

00:51:26.814 --> 00:51:30.916
how far do you need to nudge
them to really help them?

00:51:30.916 --> 00:51:34.550
And that's how we approach all
of our advertising efforts.

00:51:34.550 --> 00:51:35.977
- Yeah, that's really encouraging here,

00:51:35.977 --> 00:51:37.970
just that attitude of really listening

00:51:37.970 --> 00:51:40.670
and meeting people where
they're at in the programs

00:51:40.670 --> 00:51:41.580
that you're putting together.

00:51:41.580 --> 00:51:43.100
And it seems like it's been very effective

00:51:43.100 --> 00:51:44.990
from the statistics I've heard you share

00:51:44.990 --> 00:51:46.920
over the years as well.

00:51:46.920 --> 00:51:48.610
Dr. Fagan and Mitch, I
wonder if you'd comment

00:51:48.610 --> 00:51:50.687
on how you've also, I mean, Dr. Fagan,

00:51:50.687 --> 00:51:52.960
I'm also just thinking about
all the different things

00:51:52.960 --> 00:51:56.840
that you do and the different
layers of your communication

00:51:56.840 --> 00:51:57.673
in different communities

00:51:57.673 --> 00:52:00.581
and how you've navigated
that over the years as well.

00:52:00.581 --> 00:52:04.030
- Yeah, I wanna talk about
state legislatures, you know,

00:52:04.030 --> 00:52:06.930
because when I came out
of the federal government

00:52:06.930 --> 00:52:10.040
and started working in academia,

00:52:10.040 --> 00:52:14.040
I had to figure out how
to communicate issues

00:52:14.040 --> 00:52:17.096
around tobacco to state
legislators in Hawaii

00:52:17.096 --> 00:52:18.740
and then do it in Arkansas,

00:52:18.740 --> 00:52:20.850
which are two very different states

00:52:20.850 --> 00:52:23.790
in how you communicate that information.

00:52:23.790 --> 00:52:25.570
You know, while in Hawaii,

00:52:25.570 --> 00:52:29.430
the big issue that we were
dealing with at the time

00:52:29.430 --> 00:52:34.000
was e-cigarettes took off there

00:52:34.000 --> 00:52:36.770
early compared to other states,

00:52:36.770 --> 00:52:39.160
because their cigarette
smoking rates were down

00:52:39.160 --> 00:52:40.893
because the taxes were so high.

00:52:41.740 --> 00:52:43.720
But e-cigarettes shot up.

00:52:43.720 --> 00:52:48.720
And so we were, our research
team there started working

00:52:48.760 --> 00:52:51.010
on e-cigarettes early on,

00:52:51.010 --> 00:52:53.128
and we had some of the
first papers that come out,

00:52:53.128 --> 00:52:55.300
looking at the association

00:52:55.300 --> 00:52:57.320
between e-cigarettes
and quitting behaviors.

00:52:57.320 --> 00:52:59.410
Pallav Pokhrel was the lead author

00:52:59.410 --> 00:53:02.030
on that paper back in 2013.

00:53:02.030 --> 00:53:04.911
But we had to learn how
to communicate issues

00:53:04.911 --> 00:53:06.750
to state legislators,

00:53:06.750 --> 00:53:09.054
who were unfamiliar with products

00:53:09.054 --> 00:53:12.380
who were unfamiliar with
the issues around flavors,

00:53:12.380 --> 00:53:14.250
who weren't necessarily
familiar with issues

00:53:14.250 --> 00:53:18.070
related to menthol, you know, as a flavor.

00:53:18.070 --> 00:53:22.290
And so I had to become skilled at that.

00:53:22.290 --> 00:53:27.290
And then I went to Arkansas
where you have a different group

00:53:27.760 --> 00:53:32.046
of state legislators who
are very pro-tobacco.

00:53:32.046 --> 00:53:33.860
Hawaii was anti-tobacco,

00:53:33.860 --> 00:53:35.960
but there's still a skill
set that you had to develop

00:53:35.960 --> 00:53:37.117
to communicate things to them.

00:53:37.117 --> 00:53:40.080
And so it's a work in progress,

00:53:40.080 --> 00:53:45.080
learning how to influence
policies at the state level,

00:53:45.650 --> 00:53:48.220
educating people about the
things that need to be done

00:53:48.220 --> 00:53:51.420
to protect youth from e-cigarettes

00:53:51.420 --> 00:53:56.420
to help people to get help
with quitting tobacco products,

00:53:57.210 --> 00:54:01.490
to make sure that disparate
communities have access

00:54:01.490 --> 00:54:03.140
to nicotine replacement therapy,

00:54:03.140 --> 00:54:07.365
when they call the quit line
they can access those things.

00:54:07.365 --> 00:54:09.960
And so I think we still
have a long ways to go

00:54:09.960 --> 00:54:14.960
in terms of sharing stories across states

00:54:15.240 --> 00:54:18.910
on how you communicate
things to state legislature

00:54:18.910 --> 00:54:22.060
to make sure that we are
improving public health

00:54:22.060 --> 00:54:23.560
and not harming public health.

00:54:24.500 --> 00:54:26.150
- [Dr. Addy] Really well said.

00:54:26.150 --> 00:54:28.020
- I think for me and in
listening to what Kathy

00:54:28.020 --> 00:54:29.350
and Pebbles were saying,

00:54:29.350 --> 00:54:33.011
the thing that resonated
with me is I think about

00:54:33.011 --> 00:54:36.050
the job that I've had
for the last nine years,

00:54:36.050 --> 00:54:39.670
but having learned from
each previous position

00:54:39.670 --> 00:54:43.700
and the people I've had the
privilege of working for

00:54:43.700 --> 00:54:45.160
throughout my career.

00:54:45.160 --> 00:54:47.170
It really comes down to,
and it sounds simple,

00:54:47.170 --> 00:54:48.870
but it's actually really hard.

00:54:48.870 --> 00:54:50.580
Understand your audience.

00:54:50.580 --> 00:54:52.840
That's what I was hearing
from Kathy and Pebbles.

00:54:52.840 --> 00:54:55.340
And when you think you
understand your audience,

00:54:55.340 --> 00:54:59.711
think again, because if you think again,

00:54:59.711 --> 00:55:01.560
you may come up with something else

00:55:01.560 --> 00:55:02.847
that you hadn't thought of before.

00:55:02.847 --> 00:55:06.000
And in my job, whether
it's inside of government

00:55:06.000 --> 00:55:11.000
and having to engage with
members of Congress, staff,

00:55:14.137 --> 00:55:16.660
whoever the incumbent administration is

00:55:16.660 --> 00:55:19.730
at the department level
or at the White House,

00:55:19.730 --> 00:55:22.520
or whether it's with
any of the stakeholders

00:55:23.645 --> 00:55:26.100
that we have a
responsibility to engage with

00:55:26.100 --> 00:55:28.350
or media advocacy,

00:55:28.350 --> 00:55:31.920
understand where your
audience is coming from,

00:55:31.920 --> 00:55:33.123
what's on their mind,

00:55:34.320 --> 00:55:37.170
whether it's something that you see

00:55:37.170 --> 00:55:39.070
as things they don't understand

00:55:39.070 --> 00:55:40.760
that they need to better understand,

00:55:40.760 --> 00:55:42.480
or if they're a decision-maker,

00:55:42.480 --> 00:55:47.480
something that you're trying
to persuade them to agree to,

00:55:48.940 --> 00:55:51.560
but understanding where
they're coming from

00:55:51.560 --> 00:55:53.653
and what might be holding them back.

00:55:54.620 --> 00:55:58.320
And we spend a lot of our time trying

00:55:58.320 --> 00:56:00.740
to understand our various audiences,

00:56:00.740 --> 00:56:03.880
and the more effort we put in to that

00:56:03.880 --> 00:56:06.201
in any setting and any encounter,

00:56:06.201 --> 00:56:09.200
doesn't dramatically raise
the likelihood of success,

00:56:09.200 --> 00:56:12.777
but it makes for a much
more productive interaction.

00:56:12.777 --> 00:56:17.320
And even when we deal
with tobacco companies,

00:56:17.320 --> 00:56:18.890
there's a need for a dialogue

00:56:18.890 --> 00:56:23.160
and there's a need for a
degree of mutual understanding,

00:56:23.160 --> 00:56:25.330
even if at the end of
the day, there's way more

00:56:25.330 --> 00:56:28.010
that you might disagree
with than agree with,

00:56:28.010 --> 00:56:31.080
but just the responsibility
to understand your audience.

00:56:31.080 --> 00:56:32.700
- Yeah, and again, that really ties back

00:56:32.700 --> 00:56:35.760
into that level of humanity as well

00:56:35.760 --> 00:56:38.680
as you all have been
highlighting in your comments,

00:56:38.680 --> 00:56:40.530
maybe not necessarily with that word,

00:56:40.530 --> 00:56:42.320
but that sense has been
coming out as well.

00:56:42.320 --> 00:56:44.358
I think that's really, really important.

00:56:44.358 --> 00:56:46.130
Well, as we get ready to close,

00:56:46.130 --> 00:56:48.290
I would just also like to
give you all an opportunity

00:56:48.290 --> 00:56:50.610
to kind of have a final message

00:56:50.610 --> 00:56:53.273
to our in-person listeners
and to those who are listening

00:56:53.273 --> 00:56:55.710
on all the audio platforms.

00:56:55.710 --> 00:56:58.670
So both in terms of what
gives you encouragement,

00:56:58.670 --> 00:57:00.590
which you all have talked
about and hope moving forward

00:57:00.590 --> 00:57:05.140
and just any words of guidance
that you'd like to share

00:57:05.140 --> 00:57:06.750
just with the general audience as well

00:57:06.750 --> 00:57:08.890
around these aspects that
we've highlighted today.

00:57:08.890 --> 00:57:12.427
So we'll end on a, it's a light
question, not necessarily.

00:57:12.427 --> 00:57:14.000
But Kathy, you look like you're ready.

00:57:14.000 --> 00:57:18.430
- So I think one thing I
would love to share is that,

00:57:19.450 --> 00:57:22.580
nicotine is obviously an
incredibly addictive drug,

00:57:22.580 --> 00:57:26.716
but what I have seen is tremendous success

00:57:26.716 --> 00:57:30.390
in both preventing youth
from using the product

00:57:30.390 --> 00:57:34.320
by finding ways in that
are meaningful to them,

00:57:34.320 --> 00:57:37.280
to help them to make a different decision

00:57:37.280 --> 00:57:39.150
before they ever try a tobacco product.

00:57:39.150 --> 00:57:42.280
And despite the fact
that how addictive it is,

00:57:42.280 --> 00:57:46.740
I'm very encouraged that we know that kids

00:57:46.740 --> 00:57:51.290
once they are addicted are
very, very open to getting help.

00:57:51.290 --> 00:57:53.047
And, you know, at the federal level,

00:57:53.047 --> 00:57:55.910
the most I can do with the advertising

00:57:55.910 --> 00:57:57.350
that we develop is help them

00:57:57.350 --> 00:58:02.350
to convince them to take
a opportunity to quit.

00:58:02.830 --> 00:58:05.130
But there are so many
amazing services that

00:58:05.130 --> 00:58:06.930
if I can open the door to that

00:58:06.930 --> 00:58:10.160
and then hand them over
to, for instance, NCI,

00:58:10.160 --> 00:58:13.160
which really helps in a collaborative way

00:58:13.160 --> 00:58:15.615
to give them the tools and the techniques

00:58:15.615 --> 00:58:17.760
that they need to quit.

00:58:17.760 --> 00:58:20.360
So I guess my overall point is

00:58:20.360 --> 00:58:24.070
is that if you are in a
communications and messaging field,

00:58:24.070 --> 00:58:26.460
if you find the right
insight to the right audience

00:58:26.460 --> 00:58:28.450
and you have the right creative wrapper,

00:58:28.450 --> 00:58:30.201
you really can break through

00:58:30.201 --> 00:58:34.410
and help them live healthier lives.

00:58:34.410 --> 00:58:36.340
- Really well said. Thanks so much.

00:58:36.340 --> 00:58:37.770
Dr. Fagan.

00:58:37.770 --> 00:58:38.980
- Yeah, there are two things

00:58:38.980 --> 00:58:41.243
I wanna leave the audience with, which is,

00:58:42.460 --> 00:58:47.460
I'm very excited about our
developing new, bold leadership

00:58:48.940 --> 00:58:53.870
in the field and not just in
tobacco prevention and control,

00:58:53.870 --> 00:58:56.870
but public health and all of the sciences,

00:58:56.870 --> 00:58:58.980
because if we're looking at it

00:58:58.980 --> 00:59:01.640
from a multi-transdisciplinary approach,

00:59:01.640 --> 00:59:05.740
we need each other in order
to really reduce the toll

00:59:05.740 --> 00:59:07.850
of tobacco use in this country.

00:59:07.850 --> 00:59:12.850
And so, you know, I'm asking
that the next generation

00:59:13.520 --> 00:59:18.520
of people get engaged in
leadership opportunities to grow,

00:59:19.290 --> 00:59:23.667
to develop and take bold steps, take risk,

00:59:23.667 --> 00:59:26.260
you have nothing to lose.

00:59:26.260 --> 00:59:28.143
I can assure you of that.

00:59:29.410 --> 00:59:32.240
And when you think you
have something to lose,

00:59:32.240 --> 00:59:37.120
talk to a senior person about if that loss

00:59:37.120 --> 00:59:38.630
that you think you have to lose

00:59:38.630 --> 00:59:42.100
by taking various risk is a real loss,

00:59:42.100 --> 00:59:44.070
or is there something to be gained

00:59:44.070 --> 00:59:46.830
by that risk that you may take?

00:59:46.830 --> 00:59:49.400
I've taken risk all of my life,

00:59:49.400 --> 00:59:53.740
and it hasn't necessarily
been pretty all of the time,

00:59:53.740 --> 00:59:57.820
but I'm still here on the stage
with these wonderful people,

00:59:57.820 --> 01:00:00.910
my colleagues here, and life goes on,

01:00:00.910 --> 01:00:02.810
even when you take those risks.

01:00:02.810 --> 01:00:04.590
When you don't take the risk,

01:00:04.590 --> 01:00:07.743
you risk the chance of
nothing happening around you.

01:00:08.650 --> 01:00:09.720
That's a big risk,

01:00:09.720 --> 01:00:12.010
that nothing changes around you

01:00:12.010 --> 01:00:13.960
when you don't take the risk.

01:00:13.960 --> 01:00:18.960
And the second point I wanna make is that

01:00:19.060 --> 01:00:23.140
when it comes to reducing
these disparities

01:00:23.140 --> 01:00:28.140
in populations who have
historically been marginalized

01:00:28.799 --> 01:00:31.840
for decades and who have suffered

01:00:31.840 --> 01:00:36.560
from poverty combined with segregation,

01:00:36.560 --> 01:00:41.220
combined with being depoliticized

01:00:41.220 --> 01:00:45.000
and all those things
that cause oppression,

01:00:45.000 --> 01:00:48.842
we don't need those communities
on substances at all.

01:00:48.842 --> 01:00:53.450
And so, because they're already poor,

01:00:53.450 --> 01:00:55.800
they're already in circumstances

01:00:55.800 --> 01:00:59.380
that continue to facilitate
the addiction cycle.

01:00:59.380 --> 01:01:01.340
And so those of us who have been working

01:01:01.340 --> 01:01:03.630
in disparities research for a long time

01:01:03.630 --> 01:01:06.930
don't want our communities
on any substances.

01:01:06.930 --> 01:01:10.200
You know, we want the alcohol stores gone.

01:01:10.200 --> 01:01:12.530
We want the tobacco shops gone,

01:01:12.530 --> 01:01:16.420
and we need to leave
our communities healthy,

01:01:16.420 --> 01:01:19.730
because they have so many
things stacked against them.

01:01:19.730 --> 01:01:22.380
And that's just, you know,

01:01:22.380 --> 01:01:25.200
I know people have
different opinions about it,

01:01:25.200 --> 01:01:27.770
but those of us who worked in
health disparities research,

01:01:27.770 --> 01:01:30.450
that is kind of the perspective
that we have on that

01:01:30.450 --> 01:01:32.623
as it comes to addictive products.

01:01:33.977 --> 01:01:37.010
- Thanks for sharing so
honestly and so boldly,

01:01:37.010 --> 01:01:39.410
and also I appreciate
your encouraging words

01:01:39.410 --> 01:01:42.850
to the upcoming and
rising generation as well.

01:01:42.850 --> 01:01:44.100
Really, really well said.

01:01:45.280 --> 01:01:46.450
Mitch.

01:01:46.450 --> 01:01:48.670
- I think I'll pick up
on Pebbles' first point

01:01:48.670 --> 01:01:51.020
about be bold and take risks.

01:01:51.020 --> 01:01:52.840
I completely agree with that.

01:01:52.840 --> 01:01:57.840
But there's a lesson that
needs to be top of mind

01:02:00.810 --> 01:02:05.770
when you do that, which is
the bolder you're going to be

01:02:06.640 --> 01:02:09.620
in the field that we're in,

01:02:09.620 --> 01:02:12.970
given who and what we are up against,

01:02:12.970 --> 01:02:17.480
the harder it is to bring
about profound change.

01:02:17.480 --> 01:02:22.480
So yes, be bold and take
the risks but have patience,

01:02:23.490 --> 01:02:28.400
because the more impactful
a potential policy

01:02:28.400 --> 01:02:31.880
or a program is many
times the harder it is

01:02:31.880 --> 01:02:34.150
to see it put into place.

01:02:34.150 --> 01:02:36.992
So don't give up and have patience.

01:02:36.992 --> 01:02:40.463
In 1994 when I started working on tobacco,

01:02:41.598 --> 01:02:44.200
FDA went to the World
Conference on Tobacco or Health

01:02:44.200 --> 01:02:45.100
for the very first time.

01:02:45.100 --> 01:02:48.937
And I was one of the two
FDA-ers that got on a plane,

01:02:48.937 --> 01:02:51.470
flew to Paris, where there
was a smoking section

01:02:51.470 --> 01:02:53.980
on the plane, and we were
sitting in the last row

01:02:53.980 --> 01:02:55.040
of the non-smoking section.

01:02:55.040 --> 01:02:57.440
So a lot of good that did
on an overnight flight

01:02:57.440 --> 01:02:59.970
with the smoking section right behind us.

01:02:59.970 --> 01:03:01.103
And here we were,

01:03:01.103 --> 01:03:04.010
we were gonna tear down
walls with product regulation

01:03:04.010 --> 01:03:06.230
in 1994, we're going to
the world conference.

01:03:06.230 --> 01:03:09.600
And someone said, "You need
to go hear Nigel Gray."

01:03:09.600 --> 01:03:13.100
Nigel Gray has been, Nigel
is no longer with us,

01:03:13.100 --> 01:03:15.680
but who, a global leader in the field

01:03:15.680 --> 01:03:19.060
and very prominent member
of SRT for many, many years.

01:03:19.060 --> 01:03:21.370
They said, "You need to
go hear Nigel Gray talk."

01:03:21.370 --> 01:03:26.370
And I went to Nigel's talk
in 1994, guns a-blazing,

01:03:27.228 --> 01:03:30.690
and Nigel said what we do is so hard

01:03:30.690 --> 01:03:34.080
that he was trying to teach
lessons to those of us

01:03:34.080 --> 01:03:36.240
new to tobacco control at that time.

01:03:36.240 --> 01:03:41.050
And his message was the yard stick

01:03:41.050 --> 01:03:44.050
by which we need to
measure success or failure

01:03:44.050 --> 01:03:47.603
because of what we do is
so hard is by the decade,

01:03:48.760 --> 01:03:52.572
10-year increments,
before you know whether

01:03:52.572 --> 01:03:54.070
you have succeeded or
failed on the big stuff,

01:03:54.070 --> 01:03:55.630
not on the small stuff.

01:03:55.630 --> 01:03:58.430
And I was sitting there having none of it,

01:03:58.430 --> 01:04:03.207
because we were gonna tear
down walls really quickly

01:04:03.207 --> 01:04:08.207
and bring about this profound
change, but he was right.

01:04:08.890 --> 01:04:09.810
He was right.

01:04:09.810 --> 01:04:13.310
And so that's how I
would sync up everything

01:04:13.310 --> 01:04:15.530
that I've had the privilege of working on

01:04:15.530 --> 01:04:17.050
in my career with what Pebbles was saying

01:04:17.050 --> 01:04:19.470
about being bold and taking risks.

01:04:19.470 --> 01:04:22.010
That's how we get change made,

01:04:22.010 --> 01:04:23.760
but make sure you have the patience

01:04:23.760 --> 01:04:27.800
and the intestinal
fortitude and the dedication

01:04:27.800 --> 01:04:30.000
and the spirit that I know
everybody in this room

01:04:30.000 --> 01:04:31.710
and everybody listening
to this podcast does,

01:04:31.710 --> 01:04:33.830
because the more impactful the change,

01:04:33.830 --> 01:04:35.830
the harder is to see that change happen.

01:04:37.030 --> 01:04:38.747
- Thanks for sharing that story as well

01:04:38.747 --> 01:04:40.350
and that perspective.

01:04:40.350 --> 01:04:41.960
But one thing I just wanted to also add

01:04:41.960 --> 01:04:44.170
to every thing you all have
said is just the importance

01:04:44.170 --> 01:04:45.620
of community as well.

01:04:45.620 --> 01:04:47.820
Because one thing that
jumps out to me is the work

01:04:47.820 --> 01:04:50.530
that you all do at a community,

01:04:50.530 --> 01:04:54.700
even as you are invested in
these other communities as well.

01:04:54.700 --> 01:04:56.800
So I mean, that story touches on that,

01:04:56.800 --> 01:04:59.910
Dr. Fagan, what you've
mentioned also touches on that.

01:04:59.910 --> 01:05:02.620
And also Kathy, what
you've mentioned as well.

01:05:02.620 --> 01:05:05.340
And again, I know I'm pulling
in my mental health component,

01:05:05.340 --> 01:05:06.820
but that's so important for us to be able

01:05:06.820 --> 01:05:08.200
to be engaged in community,

01:05:08.200 --> 01:05:10.790
especially in this work
where we're wanting

01:05:10.790 --> 01:05:12.430
to see these changes so that we're able

01:05:12.430 --> 01:05:15.430
to support one another
and not get to a point

01:05:15.430 --> 01:05:17.130
where we're running 1000 miles an hour

01:05:17.130 --> 01:05:19.670
and no longer able to continue the work.

01:05:19.670 --> 01:05:21.980
So I just wanted to mention that as well.

01:05:21.980 --> 01:05:22.930
Again, just an honor,

01:05:22.930 --> 01:05:24.660
to be able to have all of you here

01:05:24.660 --> 01:05:26.050
for this in-person recording.

01:05:26.050 --> 01:05:27.730
For those who are listening,

01:05:27.730 --> 01:05:30.100
I'd also encourage you to
look up these individuals

01:05:30.100 --> 01:05:32.480
just to learn about the
impact and the efforts

01:05:32.480 --> 01:05:34.360
that they've made and
will continue to make.

01:05:34.360 --> 01:05:37.120
Mitch, and I have a feeling
that your legacy will continue,

01:05:37.120 --> 01:05:39.090
even when you're no longer
officially in the role.

01:05:39.090 --> 01:05:39.923
And maybe there'll be some way

01:05:39.923 --> 01:05:42.103
you'll also continue to be contributing,

01:05:42.103 --> 01:05:44.347
as I know many who step into retirement

01:05:44.347 --> 01:05:46.140
often find themselves doing.

01:05:46.140 --> 01:05:47.476
I'll split it that way.

01:05:47.476 --> 01:05:49.920
And thanks to everyone
who's been here in-person

01:05:49.920 --> 01:05:51.180
for this live recording.

01:05:51.180 --> 01:05:52.930
It's been an honor hosting it

01:05:52.930 --> 01:05:55.890
and hope that you all really
enjoyed the conversation,

01:05:55.890 --> 01:05:58.210
the way we're able to peel
back some of the layers,

01:05:58.210 --> 01:05:59.630
so to speak, as well.

01:05:59.630 --> 01:06:01.670
Thanks again to all of you for joining.

01:06:01.670 --> 01:06:05.110
- Thank you.
(audience applauds)

01:06:05.110 --> 01:06:07.533
- Awesome.
(soft music)

